<!-- cell: salient-concept id="85f9f6cf-2359-4f25-b182-64dbaf5f5634" title="space travel and colonization of space around earth" -->

Space travel is the crossing of the void beyond the atmosphere, and colonization is settling it with lasting human habitats; here the settled zone is the orbital space around Earth, filled with stations, shipyards and mining outposts. The story shows this frontier as a promise of escape from a planet running dry, and also as the newest prize, where the old struggles of Earth follow humanity upward.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="bec89530-5ad1-476d-b227-85a606dfffec" title="political rivalry for scarce resources on earth" -->

Political rivalry over scarce resources is the competition between states, blocs and factions for control of goods too limited to share, such as water, energy, minerals and arable land. In this story, Earth's dwindling supplies harden that rivalry into a standoff that drives the race into orbit, where every launch and every station becomes a claim in the contest.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="db25023e-7308-4ca0-95c6-aba4704ece29" title="one man's ambition and pride pushing him to seek power at all cost" -->

At the heart of the story is a single man whose ambition and wounded pride convince him that he alone should hold power, whatever it costs others. He feeds on Earth's rivalries and on the new reach of the orbital colonies, turning both into tools of his rise, and the reader wants to learn what drives him, how far he will go, and whether anyone can stop him before the frontier becomes his.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="47d4bfc2-c85f-45ff-af3f-4e166a2d5366" title="teamwork" -->

Teamwork is the cooperation of people who pool their skills, trust and effort toward a goal none of them could reach alone. In space, where one failure can doom everyone, the story shows it as a matter of survival and as the answer to the factions' rivalry and to one man's hunger to stand above all others.

<!-- cell: plot id="dc419acf-548b-4bea-b934-24a4a77c7298" title="space travel and colonization of space around earth" edges="2dd771f1-7217-4c65-8867-db22c20ba100:a98a2b54-d2dc-482d-a7ee-194aecd1e7df>74ec6464-f194-41d1-969e-45c4b3b5dc6e, 0823cf91-bb60-4ab8-b5be-17cbcb1ef64f:74ec6464-f194-41d1-969e-45c4b3b5dc6e>c7290c0f-95a2-415c-a342-0d41fba219d8, c7953f37-f0a0-4457-923e-ea3a926ef712:c7290c0f-95a2-415c-a342-0d41fba219d8>2c5fe6c6-0d7d-49ec-a08b-c551d4f1ddc5, 8a5e038b-a6e5-4d98-8230-e9387c3bb965:2c5fe6c6-0d7d-49ec-a08b-c551d4f1ddc5>35b007ad-5e65-47a8-bdb4-11f7f3083193, 55b6248e-577a-43ce-9c41-7c22dc067e77:35b007ad-5e65-47a8-bdb4-11f7f3083193>8e1fe2e4-73f6-4611-82ac-efaac37a5fd5, e87e9982-b392-468c-a628-d46c1b01e50d:8e1fe2e4-73f6-4611-82ac-efaac37a5fd5>e56d7e20-1a1b-4faa-84c2-805209a9b332, 9c666dbd-a542-4350-9833-498a6698a33b:e56d7e20-1a1b-4faa-84c2-805209a9b332>971568a7-c855-4da3-ba96-e174d67161d9, f53623d6-485c-4994-bced-3c7818c37919:971568a7-c855-4da3-ba96-e174d67161d9>e3a9e254-b63a-497a-8c2b-b437b0aaea45, 32ef61e0-2e24-4840-b751-d82470d7fd22:e3a9e254-b63a-497a-8c2b-b437b0aaea45>f0a02ddd-fbd0-4ff7-8160-b98f3e3d1e2b, b2c1cbaa-9dd1-46e7-89dd-d66bb7201f90:f0a02ddd-fbd0-4ff7-8160-b98f3e3d1e2b>afbecc6a-6d6b-4f4d-90c3-6ce43b54d550, 229321ea-b3fc-4b96-9180-529c78356918:afbecc6a-6d6b-4f4d-90c3-6ce43b54d550>881103aa-5bb2-4d42-89b4-6d38a40aaeef, a79bbf26-8fdf-4d70-b929-dce99151832e:881103aa-5bb2-4d42-89b4-6d38a40aaeef>2fae186e-46bb-434f-8c4e-d0c6c263df30, 52bdf3e4-fdbe-468a-b5fc-b9c2e999933c:2fae186e-46bb-434f-8c4e-d0c6c263df30>2fe1f60f-67ad-4672-991c-52b1ca2d4dcb, bcc458e8-9b14-4635-a366-919835d369f7:2fe1f60f-67ad-4672-991c-52b1ca2d4dcb>0a65d1dc-e41d-487b-8e38-e83d6229b9f0, d3c7b358-deb7-421f-98fd-4b2b07f3db81:0a65d1dc-e41d-487b-8e38-e83d6229b9f0>1658f8a8-18d4-417e-85da-802b99fea113" concepts="85f9f6cf-2359-4f25-b182-64dbaf5f5634" -->

<ol>
<li id="a98a2b54-d2dc-482d-a7ee-194aecd1e7df" timestamp="1" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Kessler Belt, Halcyon Ring station, arrivals deck" words="12597-13265">Teodora Vass steps off a crowded shuttle with her two sons, having sold their drought-stricken farm on the surface for passage up because the station promises shipyard work and water that never runs out, and finds Aurel Vance's salvage crew gathered on the deck: pilot Isa Morrow, engineer Dov Petrak, medic Yuna Sato and navigator Lio Banks vote to follow him to the abandoned reactor station Threnody, trusting his promise that its power will end the war between the Belt factions.</li>
<li id="74ec6464-f194-41d1-969e-45c4b3b5dc6e" timestamp="2" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard hiring hall and its adjoining records vault" words="13299-13860">Teodora learns that the family's passage up has left them owing the station for berth and recycled water, so Mateo signs a yard contract to build ore haulers for the new asteroid mining outposts and takes double shifts to clear the debt faster, while rumors spread through the hall that a surface shipping company wants to buy the station's charter. In the vault next door, Vance, back in orbit and still smarting from Geneva, has resolved to fake a war scare between the rival blocs so that frightened colonists like these will beg him to take emergency command. He searches the station and squadron files for leverage over the officer who controls cipher access and finds proof that Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran hid a forged entry in her security clearance.</li>
<li id="c7290c0f-95a2-415c-a342-0d41fba219d8" timestamp="3" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, station council chamber" words="13907-14438">With the Geneva freeze signed as an armistice barring both armies from the lithium field, the visiting Meridian Shipping Company agents announce that the Pacific bloc has launched a prospecting probe from its leased pad at Kourou toward the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX and publicly staked a survey claim on its nickel and lithium, to be worked from the Halcyon Ring. To make the station look profitable to them, the council moves up the ore hauler delivery date and cuts the gantry safety crews, so Mateo and the other indebted families' workers must take longer double shifts on unwatched gantries if they want to keep their berths and water. Meanwhile Andean coalition observers in the chamber report that their rival means to win in orbit the battery metals it lost on the ground.</li>
<li id="2c5fe6c6-0d7d-49ec-a08b-c551d4f1ddc5" timestamp="4" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard gantries" words="14476-15063">On the day the Meridian Shipping Company buys the station's charter and announces that every family's air and water will now be paid for out of yard wages, a debris strike from the Kessler Belt tears through the gantries where Teodora's elder son, Mateo, is working a double shift to finish ore haulers for the new asteroid mining outposts, and a swinging hull plate pins him. The crew save him together: Dov rebuilds the burst crane coupling, Isa flies the half-built hauler on thrusters alone to brace the plate, Lio charts a gap through the drifting wreckage and Yuna keeps the burned Dov on his feet. Vance, who only shouted orders, claims the rescue as his own, and when they sight the rival faction's ship docked at nearby Threnody he calls its crew enemies to be left behind if they get in the way, so his crew begin to doubt his promise of peace.</li>
<li id="35b007ad-5e65-47a8-bdb4-11f7f3083193" timestamp="5" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, infirmary ring hatch" words="15090-15587">Teodora hauls Mateo up the spoke elevator to the infirmary, but a Meridian Shipping Company clerk bars the hatch until she signs the station's new ledger, pledging her family's future yard wages against his care just as their old land debts on Earth were pledged against the harvest; beside the hatch, Vance reads both blocs' traffic, decrypted with the stolen cipher keys, on his wrist slate, learns that a colonial supply convoy will cross the disputed lane, and chooses that lane for his fake war scare, planning to arrive as the station's savior and force the Council to give him command, while Mara, ordered there to plot the convoy's course, watches Teodora sign and realizes the convoy's colonists will be caught in the crossfire.</li>
<li id="8e1fe2e4-73f6-4611-82ac-efaac37a5fd5" timestamp="6" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, infirmary ring" words="15611-16161">While Teodora sits beside Mateo's crushed leg, the infirmary screens relay a bulletin from the Pacific bloc's intelligence directorate in Santiago: satellite photographs show the Andean coalition fueling a capture tug at its equatorial launch complex, and the bloc's leaders, unwilling to let their rival claim 2041 KX's metals while the lithium field stays frozen, have ordered their own tug fueled and held on the pad to launch the moment the coalition's does. As the broadcast plays, Meridian Shipping Company crews, rushing to cover the bloc's launch contract, bolt meters onto every family's air and water lines and post a notice that lost shifts and medical care will be charged against household rations, and Teodora remembers selling her family's last well rights in the dust bowl to buy their berths into orbit.</li>
<li id="e56d7e20-1a1b-4faa-84c2-805209a9b332" timestamp="7" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Threnody station, Meridian ration office on the docking spine above the reactor bay" words="16198-16747">Teodora comes to the ration office to dispute Mateo's docked week, and a clerk shows her the ledger charging every family for the berths, the air, and each liter of ice the company's tugs haul in from the mining outposts. Through the viewport she watches those tugs unload beside the brown curve of Earth while Vance's loyal hands, obeying his order to strip the station, cut struts near the docking ring and weaken the hull beside the reactor bay. When ice miners in the queue curse their own docked rations, she draws them below in secret to meet Isa, Dov, Yuna, Lio and the rival engineers, where they plan to free the reactor core together, each taking the task they do best, unaware that the hull where they will work is being cut away.</li>
<li id="971568a7-c855-4da3-ba96-e174d67161d9" timestamp="8" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, water reclamation deck" words="16784-17302">When the Meridian Shipping Company meters each family's air and water against their shipyard wages and docks Teodora's elder son a week's ration for an injury he took in the yards, she gathers the miners and yard crews in the reclamation tanks to refuse the meters, seeing that the drought and debt they fled have followed them into orbit. Meanwhile Vance, still stung by the Council's snub, has sent forged intelligence to both rival blocs, each warning that the other plans to seize the colonies. As both fleets rush toward orbit, he lets the colonial supply convoy carrying the station's water be destroyed in their crossfire. When news of the loss reaches the tanks, the frightened crowd turns from refusing the meters to begging Vance's squadron to take control of Halcyon Station.</li>
<li id="e3a9e254-b63a-497a-8c2b-b437b0aaea45" timestamp="9" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard spine" words="17353-17900">The yard crews from the reclamation tank meeting walk off the half-built colony ships just as the Andean capture tug's spectral survey comes in, showing that 2041 KX holds only enough lithium to cover one side's shortage. When the Pacific bloc's craft burns to cut ahead, both governments declare any interference an act of war, and the alarmed UN summons them to Geneva. Meridian, needing its ships finished for the race, answers the strike by throttling air to the strikers' ring sections and shuttling up replacement welders from Earth. Watching the meters tick down, Teodora sees the strike will starve unless the ice miners, whose cargo feeds every breath on the station, stand with them too.</li>
<li id="f0a02ddd-fbd0-4ff7-8160-b98f3e3d1e2b" timestamp="10" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Kessler Belt, Threnody mining station, ice hauler dock" words="17946-18550">Teodora rides a returning ice hauler into Threnody's dock and persuades the ice miners, who also live on docked rations under Meridian's meters, to leave their haulers unloaded and join the shipyard strike, so the ice that feeds Halcyon Ring's air and water stops flowing and the yards fall idle. There among the idle haulers, Isa's crew and the rescued rival engineers combine Dov's seal schematics with the rivals' scans to plan a joint extraction of the station's reactor core and to share its power between both factions. When Dov refuses to hand his seal plans to Vance alone, Vance slips off to the reactor by himself, and miners, strikers and engineers alike follow to stop him.</li>
<li id="afbecc6a-6d6b-4f4d-90c3-6ce43b54d550" timestamp="11" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, central concourse beneath the Meridian executive spindle" words="18595-19219">With the ice haulers still unloaded and the station's water and air running thin, desperate striking colonists crowd the concourse and beg Vance, head of Meridian's board, to meet their demands and recall the Council's ships. He refuses, telling them Earth has abandoned them and only he can keep them alive. He diverts the last stored ice to the executive decks and to the half-built colony ship meant to carry families off the parched Earth. Then he quietly orders the meters on the strikers' ring cut, so that their fear of each other grows faster than their anger at him and the strike breaks.</li>
<li id="881103aa-5bb2-4d42-89b4-6d38a40aaeef" timestamp="12" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, habitat ring" words="19264-19749">As an Andean tug and a Pacific bloc craft, grappled to opposite sides of asteroid 2041 KX, drag it into high orbit beside Halcyon Ring, each government refuses to let go of the only metal left worth fighting for and orders the station's shipyards to build its outposts on the rock; but after weeks of strike have left the yards idle and the ice haulers from the mining outposts unloaded, Meridian cuts the air pressure to the strikers' habitat ring, just as the surface companies once cut water to the drought towns, so Teodora leads the miners and yard crews up the spokes to seize the shipyard control spindle and its air and water valves while the contested asteroid looms overhead.</li>
<li id="2fae186e-46bb-434f-8c4e-d0c6c263df30" timestamp="13" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, central spoke" words="19783-20407">As alarms climb and spin gravity fades toward the hub, Teodora's miners and yard crews climb hand over hand alongside the rival engineers and Vance's former pilot to the spindle's sealed hatch, where they pool what each knows: Teodora the seals, the rivals the coolant lines their faction kept secret, and the pilot the hull's weak points. They split the work, with the yard welders cutting through using shipbuilding torches while Teodora radios the ice haulers to turn back from the mining outposts and hold the docks. When Vance offers Teodora alone a way out over the intercom in exchange for the seal sequence, she refuses and leads the group on to the coolant junction, where Meridian's outnumbered guards, told their security cutter is still days out from Earth, surrender the spindle and its valves.</li>
<li id="2fe1f60f-67ad-4672-991c-52b1ca2d4dcb" timestamp="14" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, communications array of the shipyard control spindle" words="20446-20687">Holding the spindle, Vance restores air to the habitat ring and opens the water valves to every deck, and when the ice haulers block the docks against Meridian's security cutter from Earth, the stranded company director hands him the station codes, so the crews, who came up to escape a thirsty planet, begin drafting a charter declaring that the station's air and water belong to those who breathe and drink them. But enraged that Earth's blocs send envoys to strip him of the governorship he has declared, Vance forges transmissions blaming each bloc for the convoy's destruction, so their fleets turn on one another above the colonies while he claims every station as his own. When he orders his officers to fire on a rescue ship whose crew refuses to swear loyalty to him, they begin to whisper that he will destroy the station before he yields it.</li>
<li id="0a65d1dc-e41d-487b-8e38-e83d6229b9f0" timestamp="15" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, habitat ring commons" words="20730-21364">Meridian cuts off food shipments from Earth, and the rival surface blocs on the equatorial strait declare the strike a mutiny while each cuts power and water to its own cities to fuel a rush of night launches, their station modules and armed tugs streaking past the ring toward 2041 KX to seize the richer end of its metal; watching the launches from the commons, the miners and yard crews vote to keep striking and ration their stores, the lunar ice outposts radio that they will join and keep sending water, and the crews approve the finished charter and carry it up to the spindle for Teodora to sign.</li>
<li id="1658f8a8-18d4-417e-85da-802b99fea113" timestamp="16" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, reactor core beneath the shipyard control spindle" words="21402-21927">After the long strike by the miners and yard crews, Vance's former crew and the rival faction's engineers they once fought stabilize the failing reactor by hand, and on its console Teodora signs the charter that frees the station's air, water and power from the Meridian Shipping Company and shares them with every faction. Vance, who sealed them inside to keep the core for himself, watches alone from a locked control room that no one will open for him, while Teodora knows the company and the surface powers will now come to take the station back.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="b8c25500-f6bc-48a0-8a2a-2b1afa4b9e35" title="political rivalry for scarce resources on earth" edges="937dfcef-6fb4-4741-9477-3951b11e8f11:f214c088-72ab-4f50-a4cf-d8e7d62bd52c>248e6f4f-9663-4dce-a8bb-369a6148cd21, e4f4f678-2e9e-445b-908a-169eb1ffa552:248e6f4f-9663-4dce-a8bb-369a6148cd21>fc94f4ac-1613-490a-ad65-76bf0a43a6d5, 9fac2ab9-dfa0-4f07-a54f-970508cd972d:fc94f4ac-1613-490a-ad65-76bf0a43a6d5>1ffbfb41-acbe-42e5-aac0-3e3ab479c1c3, fd836e34-5236-4fdf-9388-c38a21bdd210:1ffbfb41-acbe-42e5-aac0-3e3ab479c1c3>29c18a49-44d7-4d24-a53c-736526903657, ef5f9694-485b-4120-82dc-efc702369685:29c18a49-44d7-4d24-a53c-736526903657>9c42da4c-20ec-4d5d-a378-2a2fc842afeb, b434180e-d4b0-4bac-8578-098aa790cd37:9c42da4c-20ec-4d5d-a378-2a2fc842afeb>849bba46-4a1d-415a-b66c-2f6b767dc39d, 8a99d1bb-1bdd-422f-bcc5-47e23b9e49a1:849bba46-4a1d-415a-b66c-2f6b767dc39d>d3264074-9653-4ae0-8fb6-ed00c78cba9e, 390fd0fb-89be-466f-9907-bbc3bd3a6c3e:d3264074-9653-4ae0-8fb6-ed00c78cba9e>fb26a9b5-8445-44e0-a016-648cc4efd1a1, ddb87814-afc2-4a99-a53c-6da499d73ad2:fb26a9b5-8445-44e0-a016-648cc4efd1a1>c1794438-8059-4659-a367-df692d8a00b2, b45aec4e-a4ac-4764-b203-1cf68860ff99:c1794438-8059-4659-a367-df692d8a00b2>a9f7e8d9-46a3-465c-ad88-5538486738d7, 4101ba09-31be-4391-b60e-ff6e918f8158:a9f7e8d9-46a3-465c-ad88-5538486738d7>1065c671-80bd-42eb-bcdb-bd85d4d338ed, 41af2a9b-7369-40a2-9901-9dec8fc2ddfc:1065c671-80bd-42eb-bcdb-bd85d4d338ed>30ed1a23-878c-44fa-a160-c9c5c1850d7a, c7e3f85a-1248-479b-b58c-2247610a6868:30ed1a23-878c-44fa-a160-c9c5c1850d7a>a15cb642-bc9d-4e37-bbcb-a192a62f2940, 431e72e6-001e-4c00-ba25-7bbd9b4580d6:a15cb642-bc9d-4e37-bbcb-a192a62f2940>2e24700f-5428-4f4c-9e7f-8f0d1412ee6b, 41f436be-590b-40b2-94bf-311806a3740f:2e24700f-5428-4f4c-9e7f-8f0d1412ee6b>3f53f60c-09af-4ca0-beef-cea6f02367bf" concepts="bec89530-5ad1-476d-b227-85a606dfffec" -->

<ol>
<li id="f214c088-72ab-4f50-a4cf-d8e7d62bd52c" timestamp="1" location="earth, Atacama salt flats, lithium concession border" words="21928-22514">With Pacific bloc and Andean coalition troops massed on either side of the last rich lithium field, each refusing to share the batteries that will decide who keeps power through the coming energy shortage, a Unified Council delegation buys a fragile truce by naming the rival bloc's candidate Governor of the Orbital Colonies over Commodore Aurel Vance, and the public humiliation before both armies convinces him that he alone deserves to rule the frontier.</li>
<li id="248e6f4f-9663-4dce-a8bb-369a6148cd21" timestamp="2" location="Kessler Belt, salvage ship Halcyon, navigation bay" words="22557-22985">A relay from the neutral treaty hall in Geneva reports that Pacific bloc and Andean coalition envoys, worn down by skirmishes along the concession border, have each rejected any plan to share the lithium field's output. The mediators could only draft a freeze that keeps both sides off the field, leaving each to seek its battery metals elsewhere. Meanwhile Lio, Isa and Dov combine star charts, flight logs and sensor tuning to find a safe long route to Threnody. But when a signal shows the rival faction's ship also heading there, Vance overrides them and orders the short cut through the debris field so he can reach the station first and claim its power for himself.</li>
<li id="fc94f4ac-1613-490a-ad65-76bf0a43a6d5" timestamp="3" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, station council chamber" words="13907-14438">With the Geneva freeze signed as an armistice barring both armies from the lithium field, the visiting Meridian Shipping Company agents announce that the Pacific bloc has launched a prospecting probe from its leased pad at Kourou toward the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX and publicly staked a survey claim on its nickel and lithium, to be worked from the Halcyon Ring. To make the station look profitable to them, the council moves up the ore hauler delivery date and cuts the gantry safety crews, so Mateo and the other indebted families' workers must take longer double shifts on unwatched gantries if they want to keep their berths and water. Meanwhile Andean coalition observers in the chamber report that their rival means to win in orbit the battery metals it lost on the ground.</li>
<li id="1ffbfb41-acbe-42e5-aac0-3e3ab479c1c3" timestamp="4" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, Vance's squadron flagship, private quarters" words="23033-23507">Still bitter at the Council's snub, Vance blackmails his intelligence officer, Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran, into handing over both rival blocs' diplomatic cipher keys. Using them, he decrypts a live feed from the Andean coalition's war council in Quito, where months of skirmishes have yielded only an armistice that bars both armies from mining the lithium field. Watching their battery reserves drain and refusing to cede the field to the Pacific bloc, the coalition's leaders order their engineers to ready a capture tug for the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX. Vance waves off Mara's warning that faking a war scare could get colonists killed.</li>
<li id="29c18a49-44d7-4d24-a53c-736526903657" timestamp="5" location="Kessler Belt, approach to Threnody, Halcyon cockpit" words="23544-24089">The Andean coalition is racing to beat the Pacific bloc to 2041 KX before the bloc's battery reserves run dry, and it has diverted the hydrogen meant for rationed city power plants to fuel its capture tug on an open pad in full view of passing satellites, so the Halcyon's patched engine, left with only dregs, cannot hold a docking burn; Isa, Lio, Dov and Yuna agree behind Vance's back to answer the rival bloc ship's hail, and its crew guide the Halcyon in with tow lines while Isa matches their every call, so the crew dock owing the rivals their lives as Vance rages at them for begging help from enemies.</li>
<li id="9c42da4c-20ec-4d5d-a378-2a2fc842afeb" timestamp="6" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, infirmary ring" words="15611-16161">While Teodora sits beside Mateo's crushed leg, the infirmary screens relay a bulletin from the Pacific bloc's intelligence directorate in Santiago: satellite photographs show the Andean coalition fueling a capture tug at its equatorial launch complex, and the bloc's leaders, unwilling to let their rival claim 2041 KX's metals while the lithium field stays frozen, have ordered their own tug fueled and held on the pad to launch the moment the coalition's does. As the broadcast plays, Meridian Shipping Company crews, rushing to cover the bloc's launch contract, bolt meters onto every family's air and water lines and post a notice that lost shifts and medical care will be charged against household rations, and Teodora remembers selling her family's last well rights in the dust bowl to buy their berths into orbit.</li>
<li id="849bba46-4a1d-415a-b66c-2f6b767dc39d" timestamp="7" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, Vance's squadron flagship, brig" words="24137-24677">With Mara locked in the brig so she cannot warn anyone, Vance watches a relayed feed from the Andean coalition's war cabinet in Quito: having learned that the Pacific bloc has fueled a tug and holds it on the pad, the ministers decide that waiting would hand their rival the asteroid's metals while the lithium field stays deadlocked, and they order their own tug launched at the next equatorial window to claim 2041 KX first. When Mara pleads that the convoy carries families, Vance answers that the colonists will thank whoever saves them. A Council envoy then hails to offer him a minor deputy seat; he takes it as one more insult, refuses, and orders the forged dispatches readied for release.</li>
<li id="d3264074-9653-4ae0-8fb6-ed00c78cba9e" timestamp="8" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, docking ring" words="24725-25241">With the lithium field on Earth frozen between their armies and neither side able to mine it, the Andean coalition's capture tug, bound for the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX to end the shortage from above, docks at Threnody to strip its reactor core and finds that the Pacific bloc, launching within hours of the coalition, has already put its own engineers aboard, turning the fight over Earth's last metals into a race for orbit. When a hull breach traps those rival engineers, Isa, Dov, Yuna and Lio defy Vance's order to leave them, and together they pull the rivals out alive: Isa holds the ship steady, Dov patches the seal, Lio guides the way and Yuna treats the wounded. Vance, watching his crew's loyalty turn toward his enemies, decides to take the reactor core for himself alone.</li>
<li id="fb26a9b5-8445-44e0-a016-648cc4efd1a1" timestamp="9" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard spine" words="17353-17900">The yard crews from the reclamation tank meeting walk off the half-built colony ships just as the Andean capture tug's spectral survey comes in, showing that 2041 KX holds only enough lithium to cover one side's shortage. When the Pacific bloc's craft burns to cut ahead, both governments declare any interference an act of war, and the alarmed UN summons them to Geneva. Meridian, needing its ships finished for the race, answers the strike by throttling air to the strikers' ring sections and shuttling up replacement welders from Earth. Watching the meters tick down, Teodora sees the strike will starve unless the ice miners, whose cargo feeds every breath on the station, stand with them too.</li>
<li id="c1794438-8059-4659-a367-df692d8a00b2" timestamp="10" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, command deck" words="25281-25741">As the Andean and Pacific craft close on 2041 KX, UN mediators speaking by link from Geneva offer the two blocs a joint mining compact, but each refuses because the asteroid's metal cannot cover both their shortages, files a sole claim and orders its crew to grapple the asteroid whatever the other does. Vance, now in command of the station, then turns away the Council's relief ships sent to replace the lost convoy, unwilling to let the body that snubbed him share the credit and determined that the colonists depend on him alone, even as their air and water begin to run out.</li>
<li id="a9f7e8d9-46a3-465c-ad88-5538486738d7" timestamp="11" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station on 2041 KX, maintenance shafts" words="25785-26336">With nickel and copper rationing closing smelters across the Andean coalition, Lima's ministers made Isa's tug burn its return-fuel reserve to reach 2041 KX first, and the Pacific bloc sent its own craft on a matching intercept rather than lose the metal. Now both crews are stranded together at the station, chasing Vance toward the reactor, and they find he has welded the hatches shut behind him. The Pacific engineers' scans chart a way around, Dov cuts the hatches open, and Isa keeps the two rival groups moving together until they reach the reactor access corridor just as Vance begins unbolting the core.</li>
<li id="1065c671-80bd-42eb-bcdb-bd85d4d338ed" timestamp="12" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, habitat ring" words="19264-19749">As an Andean tug and a Pacific bloc craft, grappled to opposite sides of asteroid 2041 KX, drag it into high orbit beside Halcyon Ring, each government refuses to let go of the only metal left worth fighting for and orders the station's shipyards to build its outposts on the rock; but after weeks of strike have left the yards idle and the ice haulers from the mining outposts unloaded, Meridian cuts the air pressure to the strikers' habitat ring, just as the surface companies once cut water to the drought towns, so Teodora leads the miners and yard crews up the spokes to seize the shipyard control spindle and its air and water valves while the contested asteroid looms overhead.</li>
<li id="30ed1a23-878c-44fa-a160-c9c5c1850d7a" timestamp="13" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, command deck" words="26377-26952">As the two locked tugs haul 2041 KX toward high orbit, Vance's officers watch the feeds from Earth: rolling blackouts spread across both blocs' cities as their lithium storage fails, the Andean council demands that the Pacific crew cut loose, and the Pacific bloc answers that its own grid will collapse without the metal. When word of Vance's rationing reaches them, the rival blocs, neither willing to wait, jointly order him to surrender the reserves and await envoys who will settle the claim and name a new governor before the asteroid arrives. Stung at being summoned like a clerk, Vance refuses, tells his officers that Earth abandoned the colonies and only he is fit to rule them, and seals the convoy's flight records so that he alone controls the story of its destruction.</li>
<li id="a15cb642-bc9d-4e37-bbcb-a192a62f2940" timestamp="14" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, reactor coolant junction" words="26994-27540">As both tugs drag 2041 KX into high Earth orbit, the two blocs' envoys meet at the station's coolant junction and reject any shared claim, because neither bloc's power grid can survive on half the metal, and each government orders its station modules and armed tugs launched to seize its own end of the asteroid; then the blast doors slam shut on them, and trapped together, Dov calls out the core's seal sequence while the rival engineers reroute coolant through lines only they know and Vance's former pilot braces a buckling bulkhead by hand, each trusting the others with their lives, until together they clear a path to the cracked core as Vance retreats alone to the control room and seals himself in.</li>
<li id="2e24700f-5428-4f4c-9e7f-8f0d1412ee6b" timestamp="15" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, habitat ring commons" words="20730-21364">Meridian cuts off food shipments from Earth, and the rival surface blocs on the equatorial strait declare the strike a mutiny while each cuts power and water to its own cities to fuel a rush of night launches, their station modules and armed tugs streaking past the ring toward 2041 KX to seize the richer end of its metal; watching the launches from the commons, the miners and yard crews vote to keep striking and ration their stores, the lunar ice outposts radio that they will join and keep sending water, and the crews approve the finished charter and carry it up to the spindle for Teodora to sign.</li>
<li id="3f53f60c-09af-4ca0-beef-cea6f02367bf" timestamp="16" location="orbit, high Earth orbit, docking ring around captured asteroid 2041 KX, Halcyon Station's governor's command deck" words="27587-28147">As the two blocs' stations lock onto opposite ends of the metal-rich asteroid, planting their flags and arming their tugs so that the standoff born over a salt flat on Earth now hangs above it with no ground left to retreat to, Vance, who seized the colonies by setting those very blocs against each other, orders Halcyon's reactor overloaded rather than surrender it, and his own officers lock him out of the controls, leaving him alone with a power no one will obey.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="fd2af35d-f450-4164-8f1a-a15c006e71c3" title="one man's ambition and pride pushing him to seek power at all cost" edges="38988c63-3430-4630-87b7-be5b35aa48d0:9d6f6468-7d70-484d-adad-9b7ff8170659>fd236728-0b8c-4ef5-bf22-db7b0439e953, 7493075f-2d3a-4d12-8d6b-727ec9d773d0:fd236728-0b8c-4ef5-bf22-db7b0439e953>e5d8f850-b4a7-4a68-b2c6-4dd2435fa9e9, e8de79ba-6967-4da5-8090-1f44c9594055:e5d8f850-b4a7-4a68-b2c6-4dd2435fa9e9>259f7802-11e7-4896-96af-7187d5c31498, bd777283-8416-4651-98b6-a6392c953921:259f7802-11e7-4896-96af-7187d5c31498>bd07180e-f07a-4d1e-a17c-4557ed380380, 8c34160b-049e-4e02-8775-61ec5b3b4e53:bd07180e-f07a-4d1e-a17c-4557ed380380>426ff637-cefe-4e45-8970-2950fa4b6c3e, 931bd41f-8f73-4ccc-b9d0-febe9c6dbcc2:426ff637-cefe-4e45-8970-2950fa4b6c3e>8d43754c-25dc-4536-bc5a-085e96852716, 90694cd5-51bd-4261-aa51-7a81731ba919:8d43754c-25dc-4536-bc5a-085e96852716>77235dd5-5de5-4db2-9bab-3bf952b06a90, 761d5de0-3f8b-44d6-b584-9cc2bfd05769:77235dd5-5de5-4db2-9bab-3bf952b06a90>b0dc97e4-0a64-4485-abc2-9209ffa6d7cd, a687368d-088b-4ed4-aeda-fcf68c03bbff:b0dc97e4-0a64-4485-abc2-9209ffa6d7cd>3c2732df-8441-4e70-a431-2d8aa05304d7, 567aab04-3839-4cc2-912c-fae4b4b0bec7:3c2732df-8441-4e70-a431-2d8aa05304d7>355e9bf1-65c9-4813-b00a-320b086309af, 91430894-a496-4977-874a-bfc2d1e312a8:355e9bf1-65c9-4813-b00a-320b086309af>77245751-5a66-49d6-b1a4-aeba188ba450, 8b286d11-c6ef-453e-898c-6c5dd645de1b:77245751-5a66-49d6-b1a4-aeba188ba450>cce2114c-84ac-4b8b-ad7d-5a72483f0cd3, 7c4a0f97-6f16-44e2-988b-95f72db9ba3d:cce2114c-84ac-4b8b-ad7d-5a72483f0cd3>50e2785e-f836-4a28-8de8-617b83ea5d79, aa282580-88f4-4a56-9ef3-835f8a938fa7:50e2785e-f836-4a28-8de8-617b83ea5d79>52ece7ed-69fb-406b-871b-bf6d328bb311, dedaf88a-e52e-4325-8ebc-eecaed1856da:52ece7ed-69fb-406b-871b-bf6d328bb311>3d71e672-79fa-4429-82f8-cb2caf57261a" concepts="db25023e-7308-4ca0-95c6-aba4704ece29" -->

<ol>
<li id="9d6f6468-7d70-484d-adad-9b7ff8170659" timestamp="1" location="earth, Atacama salt flats, lithium concession border" words="21928-22514">With Pacific bloc and Andean coalition troops massed on either side of the last rich lithium field, each refusing to share the batteries that will decide who keeps power through the coming energy shortage, a Unified Council delegation buys a fragile truce by naming the rival bloc's candidate Governor of the Orbital Colonies over Commodore Aurel Vance, and the public humiliation before both armies convinces him that he alone deserves to rule the frontier.</li>
<li id="fd236728-0b8c-4ef5-bf22-db7b0439e953" timestamp="2" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard hiring hall and its adjoining records vault" words="13299-13860">Teodora learns that the family's passage up has left them owing the station for berth and recycled water, so Mateo signs a yard contract to build ore haulers for the new asteroid mining outposts and takes double shifts to clear the debt faster, while rumors spread through the hall that a surface shipping company wants to buy the station's charter. In the vault next door, Vance, back in orbit and still smarting from Geneva, has resolved to fake a war scare between the rival blocs so that frightened colonists like these will beg him to take emergency command. He searches the station and squadron files for leverage over the officer who controls cipher access and finds proof that Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran hid a forged entry in her security clearance.</li>
<li id="e5d8f850-b4a7-4a68-b2c6-4dd2435fa9e9" timestamp="3" location="Kessler Belt, debris field outside Threnody, Halcyon, bridge" words="28148-28638">As the Halcyon follows Vance's shortcut into the debris field, a Council broadcast on the bridge praises his rival's peace talks without naming him, and Vance, seething, copies Oyelaran's forged clearance file to his private drive and orders her to report to his quarters alone that night. Meanwhile Lio calls out drifting wreckage, Isa weaves the ship between it, and Yuna relays Dov's warning that the engine coupling is overheating, but Vance, fearing the rival faction's ship will reach Threnody first, overrules them and orders full speed, pushing the ship into a dense cluster of wreckage.</li>
<li id="259f7802-11e7-4896-96af-7187d5c31498" timestamp="4" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, Vance's squadron flagship, private quarters" words="23033-23507">Still bitter at the Council's snub, Vance blackmails his intelligence officer, Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran, into handing over both rival blocs' diplomatic cipher keys. Using them, he decrypts a live feed from the Andean coalition's war council in Quito, where months of skirmishes have yielded only an armistice that bars both armies from mining the lithium field. Watching their battery reserves drain and refusing to cede the field to the Pacific bloc, the coalition's leaders order their engineers to ready a capture tug for the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX. Vance waves off Mara's warning that faking a war scare could get colonists killed.</li>
<li id="bd07180e-f07a-4d1e-a17c-4557ed380380" timestamp="5" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, infirmary ring hatch" words="15090-15587">Teodora hauls Mateo up the spoke elevator to the infirmary, but a Meridian Shipping Company clerk bars the hatch until she signs the station's new ledger, pledging her family's future yard wages against his care just as their old land debts on Earth were pledged against the harvest; beside the hatch, Vance reads both blocs' traffic, decrypted with the stolen cipher keys, on his wrist slate, learns that a colonial supply convoy will cross the disputed lane, and chooses that lane for his fake war scare, planning to arrive as the station's savior and force the Council to give him command, while Mara, ordered there to plot the convoy's course, watches Teodora sign and realizes the convoy's colonists will be caught in the crossfire.</li>
<li id="426ff637-cefe-4e45-8970-2950fa4b6c3e" timestamp="6" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, salvage corridor" words="28683-29013">With Vance's flagship docked at Threnody, the crew meet the rival faction's engineers and find them tired salvagers, not enemies. Lio swaps charts with their navigator, Dov shares tools with their chief, and both crews learn the reactor core can only be freed if they work as one. Meanwhile Vance uses the stolen cipher keys to write forged dispatches in each bloc's code himself. He catches Mara using the station relay to secretly warn a colonial supply convoy, strips her access, and reroutes the convoy into the coming battle zone, telling himself the Council will regret having passed him over. He then forbids any dealings with the rivals and orders his crew to strip the station first, so they come to trust the rivals more than him.</li>
<li id="8d43754c-25dc-4536-bc5a-085e96852716" timestamp="7" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, Vance's squadron flagship, brig" words="24137-24677">With Mara locked in the brig so she cannot warn anyone, Vance watches a relayed feed from the Andean coalition's war cabinet in Quito: having learned that the Pacific bloc has fueled a tug and holds it on the pad, the ministers decide that waiting would hand their rival the asteroid's metals while the lithium field stays deadlocked, and they order their own tug launched at the next equatorial window to claim 2041 KX first. When Mara pleads that the convoy carries families, Vance answers that the colonists will thank whoever saves them. A Council envoy then hails to offer him a minor deputy seat; he takes it as one more insult, refuses, and orders the forged dispatches readied for release.</li>
<li id="77235dd5-5de5-4db2-9bab-3bf952b06a90" timestamp="8" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, water reclamation deck" words="16784-17302">When the Meridian Shipping Company meters each family's air and water against their shipyard wages and docks Teodora's elder son a week's ration for an injury he took in the yards, she gathers the miners and yard crews in the reclamation tanks to refuse the meters, seeing that the drought and debt they fled have followed them into orbit. Meanwhile Vance, still stung by the Council's snub, has sent forged intelligence to both rival blocs, each warning that the other plans to seize the colonies. As both fleets rush toward orbit, he lets the colonial supply convoy carrying the station's water be destroyed in their crossfire. When news of the loss reaches the tanks, the frightened crowd turns from refusing the meters to begging Vance's squadron to take control of Halcyon Station.</li>
<li id="b0dc97e4-0a64-4485-abc2-9209ffa6d7cd" timestamp="9" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, medical bay" words="29048-29630">With the convoy's wreckage still drifting past the viewports, Yuna tends the wounds of the rescued rival engineers, who repay their rescuers by revealing their scans of the unstable reactor core. Isa, Dov, Yuna and Lio see that neither crew can extract it alone, since the rivals know where the core is weak and Dov knows how to seal it, so they agree to meet in the salvage bay and plan together. Then the terrified colonial assembly crowds in and offers Vance emergency command of the station. He accepts only after making them renounce the Council's authority and swear that no one but him will speak for Halcyon, and as the room empties he quietly orders Dov to bring his seal plans to him alone.</li>
<li id="3c2732df-8441-4e70-a431-2d8aa05304d7" timestamp="10" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, command deck" words="25281-25741">As the Andean and Pacific craft close on 2041 KX, UN mediators speaking by link from Geneva offer the two blocs a joint mining compact, but each refuses because the asteroid's metal cannot cover both their shortages, files a sole claim and orders its crew to grapple the asteroid whatever the other does. Vance, now in command of the station, then turns away the Council's relief ships sent to replace the lost convoy, unwilling to let the body that snubbed him share the credit and determined that the colonists depend on him alone, even as their air and water begin to run out.</li>
<li id="355e9bf1-65c9-4813-b00a-320b086309af" timestamp="11" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, central concourse beneath the Meridian executive spindle" words="18595-19219">With the ice haulers still unloaded and the station's water and air running thin, desperate striking colonists crowd the concourse and beg Vance, head of Meridian's board, to meet their demands and recall the Council's ships. He refuses, telling them Earth has abandoned them and only he can keep them alive. He diverts the last stored ice to the executive decks and to the half-built colony ship meant to carry families off the parched Earth. Then he quietly orders the meters on the strikers' ring cut, so that their fear of each other grows faster than their anger at him and the strike breaks.</li>
<li id="77245751-5a66-49d6-b1a4-aeba188ba450" timestamp="12" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, life-support depot reactor corridor" words="29674-30285">With the supply convoy destroyed and the colonists who begged for his protection fighting each other for dwindling air and water, Vance seizes the reserves, grants rations only to those who swear loyalty, and declares himself governor while his officers watch him starve the people he claimed to save. He then tries to pull the depot's reactor core out alone, but without Dov's knowledge of its seals he cracks the containment. To keep the core his, he locks the blast doors, trapping his former crew and the rescued rival engineers inside, where they agree to pool their skills to stop the meltdown none of them could stop alone.</li>
<li id="cce2114c-84ac-4b8b-ad7d-5a72483f0cd3" timestamp="13" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, command deck" words="26377-26952">As the two locked tugs haul 2041 KX toward high orbit, Vance's officers watch the feeds from Earth: rolling blackouts spread across both blocs' cities as their lithium storage fails, the Andean council demands that the Pacific crew cut loose, and the Pacific bloc answers that its own grid will collapse without the metal. When word of Vance's rationing reaches them, the rival blocs, neither willing to wait, jointly order him to surrender the reserves and await envoys who will settle the claim and name a new governor before the asteroid arrives. Stung at being summoned like a clerk, Vance refuses, tells his officers that Earth abandoned the colonies and only he is fit to rule them, and seals the convoy's flight records so that he alone controls the story of its destruction.</li>
<li id="50e2785e-f836-4a28-8de8-617b83ea5d79" timestamp="14" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, communications array of the shipyard control spindle" words="20446-20687">Holding the spindle, Vance restores air to the habitat ring and opens the water valves to every deck, and when the ice haulers block the docks against Meridian's security cutter from Earth, the stranded company director hands him the station codes, so the crews, who came up to escape a thirsty planet, begin drafting a charter declaring that the station's air and water belong to those who breathe and drink them. But enraged that Earth's blocs send envoys to strip him of the governorship he has declared, Vance forges transmissions blaming each bloc for the convoy's destruction, so their fleets turn on one another above the colonies while he claims every station as his own. When he orders his officers to fire on a rescue ship whose crew refuses to swear loyalty to him, they begin to whisper that he will destroy the station before he yields it.</li>
<li id="52ece7ed-69fb-406b-871b-bf6d328bb311" timestamp="15" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, reactor core antechamber" words="30327-30868">When the battered bloc fleets uncover Vance's forged transmissions and offer amnesty to any officer who hands him over, his pride will not let him bargain, so he has the reactor's safety limits stripped and declares he will burn the station before anyone else rules it; while his chief engineer quietly reroutes the controls behind his back, the core cracks, and its manual seal needs three valves turned at once on opposite sides of the chamber, so Dov counts the rhythm over the comm as a rival engineer and Vance's former pilot crawl through the radiation haze on a single shared tether, and when Vance vents the antechamber's air to stop them they pass one oxygen mask hand to hand until the first valve turns.</li>
<li id="3d71e672-79fa-4429-82f8-cb2caf57261a" timestamp="16" location="orbit, high Earth orbit, docking ring around captured asteroid 2041 KX, Halcyon Station's governor's command deck" words="27587-28147">As the two blocs' stations lock onto opposite ends of the metal-rich asteroid, planting their flags and arming their tugs so that the standoff born over a salt flat on Earth now hangs above it with no ground left to retreat to, Vance, who seized the colonies by setting those very blocs against each other, orders Halcyon's reactor overloaded rather than surrender it, and his own officers lock him out of the controls, leaving him alone with a power no one will obey.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="0bd31b8f-0fce-4a7b-b9bc-130ec189f5a4" title="teamwork" edges="3e50e882-1c17-4f38-8a7c-ba584607f12d:470c76c3-43bd-40bd-b85d-41da93f5b999>03c5d264-a99b-4a66-b328-da41d4213d85, 3346eabc-5b94-4958-ad4e-dd7249c6c7ba:03c5d264-a99b-4a66-b328-da41d4213d85>eca7e526-35c0-48b2-9586-d41b5ec0e914, d967c919-1077-4b24-97c8-ad465a164b4f:eca7e526-35c0-48b2-9586-d41b5ec0e914>50118912-30cb-4037-a530-8cafa8ea1faa, 7cf10033-646f-4868-ad75-92ad76f51acc:50118912-30cb-4037-a530-8cafa8ea1faa>dab76326-8506-48e7-9eeb-342ead0b48c3, 4cbf451a-dc98-481b-80e6-a70df680e2e1:dab76326-8506-48e7-9eeb-342ead0b48c3>cf982e6a-5bfe-4c7c-9a24-209e30abd02b, 6063efc9-5b98-4f50-bd41-95b44c730995:cf982e6a-5bfe-4c7c-9a24-209e30abd02b>2a42b3d3-8f4d-497c-89c2-8da9b7b4c856, d5870bf9-2e5e-431e-8f14-e9f090614723:2a42b3d3-8f4d-497c-89c2-8da9b7b4c856>37105d8f-c4cb-4473-8860-79eb6038fbb5, f6dfb233-c263-4651-a03b-ef040c5107ba:37105d8f-c4cb-4473-8860-79eb6038fbb5>d92d899f-0dbd-4576-ba47-df9874b5fcf2, 21b457c4-6ebd-4e33-9044-9099d736c40f:d92d899f-0dbd-4576-ba47-df9874b5fcf2>960a63c7-7deb-4f74-a9fc-09d29c57542c, 1fb16b67-57a7-41ef-945d-e85aa73033f8:960a63c7-7deb-4f74-a9fc-09d29c57542c>e4386aaf-8089-42f7-932d-740e171b1dd6, 2ecac8d9-2f7a-4391-917c-c94c7afd3781:e4386aaf-8089-42f7-932d-740e171b1dd6>5862f56b-d42c-4960-8d35-ad4c7a4a07a2, e83a1e92-edda-407a-9c3a-b9377fc09e38:5862f56b-d42c-4960-8d35-ad4c7a4a07a2>2856b37f-4e8d-483b-821d-6dc013941f8f, c0c0d4a2-c3bd-4750-8bce-0f478c81ecba:2856b37f-4e8d-483b-821d-6dc013941f8f>2cf92a04-accd-4a20-8dc2-93129c6daf4f, 9a97ec95-b09a-427b-85bf-1889ba77d635:2cf92a04-accd-4a20-8dc2-93129c6daf4f>b8468d2c-bc0e-4220-a535-e1fe42fa827e, 01021063-c18c-4e01-bedf-de9d09eee510:b8468d2c-bc0e-4220-a535-e1fe42fa827e>ea1d7936-f5fe-4d30-8453-00877307b95f" concepts="47d4bfc2-c85f-45ff-af3f-4e166a2d5366" -->

<ol>
<li id="470c76c3-43bd-40bd-b85d-41da93f5b999" timestamp="1" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Kessler Belt, Halcyon Ring station, arrivals deck" words="12597-13265">Teodora Vass steps off a crowded shuttle with her two sons, having sold their drought-stricken farm on the surface for passage up because the station promises shipyard work and water that never runs out, and finds Aurel Vance's salvage crew gathered on the deck: pilot Isa Morrow, engineer Dov Petrak, medic Yuna Sato and navigator Lio Banks vote to follow him to the abandoned reactor station Threnody, trusting his promise that its power will end the war between the Belt factions.</li>
<li id="03c5d264-a99b-4a66-b328-da41d4213d85" timestamp="2" location="Kessler Belt, salvage ship Halcyon, navigation bay" words="22557-22985">A relay from the neutral treaty hall in Geneva reports that Pacific bloc and Andean coalition envoys, worn down by skirmishes along the concession border, have each rejected any plan to share the lithium field's output. The mediators could only draft a freeze that keeps both sides off the field, leaving each to seek its battery metals elsewhere. Meanwhile Lio, Isa and Dov combine star charts, flight logs and sensor tuning to find a safe long route to Threnody. But when a signal shows the rival faction's ship also heading there, Vance overrides them and orders the short cut through the debris field so he can reach the station first and claim its power for himself.</li>
<li id="eca7e526-35c0-48b2-9586-d41b5ec0e914" timestamp="3" location="Kessler Belt, debris field outside Threnody, Halcyon, bridge" words="28148-28638">As the Halcyon follows Vance's shortcut into the debris field, a Council broadcast on the bridge praises his rival's peace talks without naming him, and Vance, seething, copies Oyelaran's forged clearance file to his private drive and orders her to report to his quarters alone that night. Meanwhile Lio calls out drifting wreckage, Isa weaves the ship between it, and Yuna relays Dov's warning that the engine coupling is overheating, but Vance, fearing the rival faction's ship will reach Threnody first, overrules them and orders full speed, pushing the ship into a dense cluster of wreckage.</li>
<li id="50118912-30cb-4037-a530-8cafa8ea1faa" timestamp="4" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, shipyard gantries" words="14476-15063">On the day the Meridian Shipping Company buys the station's charter and announces that every family's air and water will now be paid for out of yard wages, a debris strike from the Kessler Belt tears through the gantries where Teodora's elder son, Mateo, is working a double shift to finish ore haulers for the new asteroid mining outposts, and a swinging hull plate pins him. The crew save him together: Dov rebuilds the burst crane coupling, Isa flies the half-built hauler on thrusters alone to brace the plate, Lio charts a gap through the drifting wreckage and Yuna keeps the burned Dov on his feet. Vance, who only shouted orders, claims the rescue as his own, and when they sight the rival faction's ship docked at nearby Threnody he calls its crew enemies to be left behind if they get in the way, so his crew begin to doubt his promise of peace.</li>
<li id="dab76326-8506-48e7-9eeb-342ead0b48c3" timestamp="5" location="Kessler Belt, approach to Threnody, Halcyon cockpit" words="23544-24089">The Andean coalition is racing to beat the Pacific bloc to 2041 KX before the bloc's battery reserves run dry, and it has diverted the hydrogen meant for rationed city power plants to fuel its capture tug on an open pad in full view of passing satellites, so the Halcyon's patched engine, left with only dregs, cannot hold a docking burn; Isa, Lio, Dov and Yuna agree behind Vance's back to answer the rival bloc ship's hail, and its crew guide the Halcyon in with tow lines while Isa matches their every call, so the crew dock owing the rivals their lives as Vance rages at them for begging help from enemies.</li>
<li id="cf982e6a-5bfe-4c7c-9a24-209e30abd02b" timestamp="6" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, salvage corridor" words="28683-29013">With Vance's flagship docked at Threnody, the crew meet the rival faction's engineers and find them tired salvagers, not enemies. Lio swaps charts with their navigator, Dov shares tools with their chief, and both crews learn the reactor core can only be freed if they work as one. Meanwhile Vance uses the stolen cipher keys to write forged dispatches in each bloc's code himself. He catches Mara using the station relay to secretly warn a colonial supply convoy, strips her access, and reroutes the convoy into the coming battle zone, telling himself the Council will regret having passed him over. He then forbids any dealings with the rivals and orders his crew to strip the station first, so they come to trust the rivals more than him.</li>
<li id="2a42b3d3-8f4d-497c-89c2-8da9b7b4c856" timestamp="7" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Threnody station, Meridian ration office on the docking spine above the reactor bay" words="16198-16747">Teodora comes to the ration office to dispute Mateo's docked week, and a clerk shows her the ledger charging every family for the berths, the air, and each liter of ice the company's tugs haul in from the mining outposts. Through the viewport she watches those tugs unload beside the brown curve of Earth while Vance's loyal hands, obeying his order to strip the station, cut struts near the docking ring and weaken the hull beside the reactor bay. When ice miners in the queue curse their own docked rations, she draws them below in secret to meet Isa, Dov, Yuna, Lio and the rival engineers, where they plan to free the reactor core together, each taking the task they do best, unaware that the hull where they will work is being cut away.</li>
<li id="37105d8f-c4cb-4473-8860-79eb6038fbb5" timestamp="8" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, docking ring" words="24725-25241">With the lithium field on Earth frozen between their armies and neither side able to mine it, the Andean coalition's capture tug, bound for the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX to end the shortage from above, docks at Threnody to strip its reactor core and finds that the Pacific bloc, launching within hours of the coalition, has already put its own engineers aboard, turning the fight over Earth's last metals into a race for orbit. When a hull breach traps those rival engineers, Isa, Dov, Yuna and Lio defy Vance's order to leave them, and together they pull the rivals out alive: Isa holds the ship steady, Dov patches the seal, Lio guides the way and Yuna treats the wounded. Vance, watching his crew's loyalty turn toward his enemies, decides to take the reactor core for himself alone.</li>
<li id="d92d899f-0dbd-4576-ba47-df9874b5fcf2" timestamp="9" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, medical bay" words="29048-29630">With the convoy's wreckage still drifting past the viewports, Yuna tends the wounds of the rescued rival engineers, who repay their rescuers by revealing their scans of the unstable reactor core. Isa, Dov, Yuna and Lio see that neither crew can extract it alone, since the rivals know where the core is weak and Dov knows how to seal it, so they agree to meet in the salvage bay and plan together. Then the terrified colonial assembly crowds in and offers Vance emergency command of the station. He accepts only after making them renounce the Council's authority and swear that no one but him will speak for Halcyon, and as the room empties he quietly orders Dov to bring his seal plans to him alone.</li>
<li id="960a63c7-7deb-4f74-a9fc-09d29c57542c" timestamp="10" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Kessler Belt, Threnody mining station, ice hauler dock" words="17946-18550">Teodora rides a returning ice hauler into Threnody's dock and persuades the ice miners, who also live on docked rations under Meridian's meters, to leave their haulers unloaded and join the shipyard strike, so the ice that feeds Halcyon Ring's air and water stops flowing and the yards fall idle. There among the idle haulers, Isa's crew and the rescued rival engineers combine Dov's seal schematics with the rivals' scans to plan a joint extraction of the station's reactor core and to share its power between both factions. When Dov refuses to hand his seal plans to Vance alone, Vance slips off to the reactor by himself, and miners, strikers and engineers alike follow to stop him.</li>
<li id="e4386aaf-8089-42f7-932d-740e171b1dd6" timestamp="11" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station on 2041 KX, maintenance shafts" words="25785-26336">With nickel and copper rationing closing smelters across the Andean coalition, Lima's ministers made Isa's tug burn its return-fuel reserve to reach 2041 KX first, and the Pacific bloc sent its own craft on a matching intercept rather than lose the metal. Now both crews are stranded together at the station, chasing Vance toward the reactor, and they find he has welded the hatches shut behind him. The Pacific engineers' scans chart a way around, Dov cuts the hatches open, and Isa keeps the two rival groups moving together until they reach the reactor access corridor just as Vance begins unbolting the core.</li>
<li id="5862f56b-d42c-4960-8d35-ad4c7a4a07a2" timestamp="12" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, life-support depot reactor corridor" words="29674-30285">With the supply convoy destroyed and the colonists who begged for his protection fighting each other for dwindling air and water, Vance seizes the reserves, grants rations only to those who swear loyalty, and declares himself governor while his officers watch him starve the people he claimed to save. He then tries to pull the depot's reactor core out alone, but without Dov's knowledge of its seals he cracks the containment. To keep the core his, he locks the blast doors, trapping his former crew and the rescued rival engineers inside, where they agree to pool their skills to stop the meltdown none of them could stop alone.</li>
<li id="2856b37f-4e8d-483b-821d-6dc013941f8f" timestamp="13" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, central spoke" words="19783-20407">As alarms climb and spin gravity fades toward the hub, Teodora's miners and yard crews climb hand over hand alongside the rival engineers and Vance's former pilot to the spindle's sealed hatch, where they pool what each knows: Teodora the seals, the rivals the coolant lines their faction kept secret, and the pilot the hull's weak points. They split the work, with the yard welders cutting through using shipbuilding torches while Teodora radios the ice haulers to turn back from the mining outposts and hold the docks. When Vance offers Teodora alone a way out over the intercom in exchange for the seal sequence, she refuses and leads the group on to the coolant junction, where Meridian's outnumbered guards, told their security cutter is still days out from Earth, surrender the spindle and its valves.</li>
<li id="2cf92a04-accd-4a20-8dc2-93129c6daf4f" timestamp="14" location="Kessler Belt, Threnody station, reactor coolant junction" words="26994-27540">As both tugs drag 2041 KX into high Earth orbit, the two blocs' envoys meet at the station's coolant junction and reject any shared claim, because neither bloc's power grid can survive on half the metal, and each government orders its station modules and armed tugs launched to seize its own end of the asteroid; then the blast doors slam shut on them, and trapped together, Dov calls out the core's seal sequence while the rival engineers reroute coolant through lines only they know and Vance's former pilot braces a buckling bulkhead by hand, each trusting the others with their lives, until together they clear a path to the cracked core as Vance retreats alone to the control room and seals himself in.</li>
<li id="b8468d2c-bc0e-4220-a535-e1fe42fa827e" timestamp="15" location="orbit, Halcyon Station, reactor core antechamber" words="30327-30868">When the battered bloc fleets uncover Vance's forged transmissions and offer amnesty to any officer who hands him over, his pride will not let him bargain, so he has the reactor's safety limits stripped and declares he will burn the station before anyone else rules it; while his chief engineer quietly reroutes the controls behind his back, the core cracks, and its manual seal needs three valves turned at once on opposite sides of the chamber, so Dov counts the rhythm over the comm as a rival engineer and Vance's former pilot crawl through the radiation haze on a single shared tether, and when Vance vents the antechamber's air to stop them they pass one oxygen mask hand to hand until the first valve turns.</li>
<li id="ea1d7936-f5fe-4d30-8453-00877307b95f" timestamp="16" location="near-earth space, high orbit, Halcyon Ring station, reactor core beneath the shipyard control spindle" words="21402-21927">After the long strike by the miners and yard crews, Vance's former crew and the rival faction's engineers they once fought stabilize the failing reactor by hand, and on its console Teodora signs the charter that frees the station's air, water and power from the Meridian Shipping Company and shares them with every faction. Vance, who sealed them inside to keep the core for himself, watches alone from a locked control room that no one will open for him, while Teodora knows the company and the surface powers will now come to take the station back.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="48e5dbfe-bc6c-4a1a-b860-c5408841eb40" -->

The shuttle's hatch sighed open onto the arrivals deck of Halcyon Ring, and the passengers spilled out all at once, pressed shoulder to shoulder, carrying everything they owned in duffels and crates and plastic sacks. Teodora Vass came out in the middle of the crush with a hand on each of her sons, the older one nearly as tall as she was and the younger still small enough to be steered by the collar. The air smelled of ozone and recycled breath. Above the customs gates a banner turned slowly in the spin-gravity draft: SHIPYARD BERTHS HIRING DAILY, and beneath it, in letters nearly as large, WATER WITHOUT RATION. Teodora read it twice. The money from the farm, from the cracked fields and the dry well and the house her grandfather had built, was sewn into the lining of her coat, and what was left of it would not buy much more than a week of bunk space.

The younger boy stopped walking, and the crowd broke around them like water around a stone. He was staring past the gates toward a bay where the deck lights had been left at half strength, where five people stood in a loose ring beside a scarred salvage tug. A tall man with a shaved head and a coat patched at both elbows stood at the center of them. His voice carried, not loud but steady, over the clatter of the deck. "Threnody's core is intact," he was saying. "I've seen the telemetry. Whoever holds that much power holds the Belt, and nobody fights over air and water once there's enough of both. We bring it back online, and the war's over. Not paused. Over."

The four around him did not answer right away. A woman in a pilot's flight jacket, her hair cropped close, looked at the tug and then back at him. "Aurel, that station's been dark for eleven years." Beside her, a heavyset man with grease worked into the creases of his knuckles folded his arms and said nothing. A slight woman with a medic's kit slung across her chest glanced at the younger man next to her, who had a navigator's slate tucked under one arm and was chewing the inside of his cheek. "Then it's waiting for us," Aurel Vance said. "Isa. Dov. Yuna. Lio. I'm not ordering anyone. Hands."

Isa Morrow raised hers first, slowly, as though it were heavier than it looked. Dov Petrak unfolded his arms and lifted one thick hand without a word. Yuna Sato's went up next, and Lio Banks raised his last, the slate sliding against his ribs as he did. Aurel looked at each of them in turn and nodded once, and the engineer was already moving toward the tug's cargo ramp. Teodora watched them a moment longer than she meant to, her fingers tightening on her younger son's collar. Then the queue for the customs gates shifted forward, someone behind her muttered at her to move, and she turned her boys toward the banner and the promise of water and walked on.

<!-- cell: markdown id="7a2e416f-4b87-4c81-932f-f4140113b8d0" -->

The hiring hall of Halcyon Ring smelled of welding flux and too many bodies breathing the same air. Teodora stood at the clerk's window with a ration chit in one hand and the family's arrival papers in the other, and she read the figure on the screen twice before she understood it. Berth fees from the hour they had docked. Water, reclaimed and metered by the liter, every cup the children had drunk since. The passage up had been paid, the clerk explained without looking at her, but living on the station was another matter, and the station kept its own books. Behind her the line shifted and muttered. Somewhere a loader's alarm bleated through the bulkhead.

Mateo did not argue. He took the stylus from the clerk's tray and read the yard contract standing up: ore haulers for the new asteroid outposts, hull framing and seal work, piece rate with a bonus for speed. He asked whether a man could take two shifts back to back. The clerk said a man could take as many as the foreman would let him, and Mateo signed for double. Teodora watched the debt column on the screen tick down by a projected sliver, then watched him fold the copy into his shirt pocket as if it weighed nothing. Around them the talk in the hall had turned. A surface shipping company wanted the station's charter, someone said, and someone else said the price was already agreed, and a third voice said that if the Ring were sold, every contract signed today would be paper in a recycler by spring. Nobody knew who had started it. Everybody repeated it.

Through the wall, in the records vault, the noise of the hall arrived only as a low pressure against the ears. Commander Vance sat alone among the cold racks with a borrowed access key and a grudge he had carried up from Geneva like ballast. He had heard the rumors through the door and found them useful, for a frightened station was a pliant one. Give these people a war, or the convincing shadow of one, two blocs bristling at each other across the orbital lanes, and the same colonists now queuing for water credit would come to him asking that someone, anyone, take emergency command. But a war scare needed signals, and signals needed the ciphers, and the ciphers answered to a single officer.

He went through the station personnel files first, then the squadron's, scrolling past commendations and medical waivers and leave records until his eyes ached in the blue glare. It was in the security clearance archive that he stopped. Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran, cipher custodian. Her vetting history carried a background entry whose verification stamp predated the office that had supposedly issued it. Vance pulled the original submission, compared the routing codes, and checked them a third time against the archive index. The entry was forged, cleanly and carefully, and it had sat unnoticed for years. He copied it to a private slate, closed the file exactly as he had found it, and sat for a long moment in the hum of the racks, listening to the hall beyond the wall talk itself into fear.

<!-- cell: markdown id="f183d339-a6ef-42c9-b9e7-3eaf20999255" -->

The council chamber of the Halcyon Ring sat at the station's hub, where the spin was gentlest and the water ration went furthest, and on the morning the Geneva freeze reached orbit the long curved table was fuller than its chairs. The text of the armistice glowed on the wall screen in four languages: neither army would set foot in the lithium field again, and the trucks, the drills, and the men who guarded them were to stand down where they were. Some of the councillors had read it twice. Behind them, along the observers' rail, two delegates of the Andean coalition stood in pressed gray jackets and said nothing while the room murmured over the clauses.

The Meridian Shipping Company agents waited until the murmuring stopped. There were three of them, in soft-soled boots that made no sound on the deck plates, and the eldest set a slate on the table and turned it so the councillors could see. The Pacific bloc, she said, had launched a prospecting probe from its leased pad at Kourou six hours earlier, bound for the asteroid 2041 KX. A survey claim on the rock's nickel and lithium had been filed publicly within the hour, and the filing named the Halcyon Ring as the station from which the claim would be worked. Meridian was prepared to carry the ore, she said, if the Ring could show it was worth the freight. She did not say what would happen if it could not. She folded her hands and let the council work that out.

The council worked it out before the agents had finished their tea. The ore hauler, promised for the end of the next quarter, would be delivered at the end of this one, and to pay for the speed the gantry safety crews would be cut by half. The line was read into the record in a dry voice, and on the far side of the chamber, where the debtor families were permitted to stand during open sessions, Mateo Quispe heard it read and understood it at once. The men and women who owed the Ring for their passage, their air, and their berths would work double shifts on the outer gantries, longer than before, with no spotter on the tether line and no one at the winch if a clamp slipped. Those who refused could surrender their berth tokens and their water cards at the quartermaster's hatch. Nobody near Mateo spoke. A woman beside him closed her fingers around the card on the cord at her neck, as if someone might take it then and there.

When the agenda reached its end, the senior Andean observer asked to be heard, and the chairman, glancing at the Meridian agents, allowed it. The armistice was genuine, the observer said, and his government would honor it. But his government had also watched the launch from Kourou, and it had no doubt what the launch meant. The Pacific bloc had lost the battery metals on the ground, at the table in Geneva, and it intended to take them back in orbit, where no armistice reached and no army needed to march. He thanked the council for its hospitality and sat down. On the wall the treaty text kept glowing in its four languages, and beneath it someone had already pinned the new shift roster, with Mateo's name printed twice in a single day.

<!-- cell: markdown id="023f8617-462b-444e-bd29-a8d42e32b5cc" -->

The announcement had gone out across every deck of Halcyon Ring at the start of the second shift: the Meridian Shipping Company now held the station's charter, and from this day forward each family's air and water would be drawn from yard wages, metered and billed. The words still scrolled across the visor displays of the gantry crews when the Kessler Belt sent its answer. A spray of old satellite fragments, bolts and frozen coolant came through the shipyard at eleven kilometers a second, and the gantries rang like struck bells. Teodora's elder son, Mateo, halfway through a double shift on the ribs of an ore hauler meant for the new asteroid outposts, turned at the sound in time to see a hull plate the size of a chapel door tear loose from its crane, swing on one cable, and fold him against the frame.

Vance's voice filled every channel at once, shouting for the crane, for the hauler, for somebody, for anybody, while the crew moved around the noise of him. Dov reached the burst coupling first and stripped it down with his gloves smoking where a severed line had sprayed him, rebuilding the joint from parts pulled off the dead crane beside it. Yuna braced herself behind him and held him upright by the harness when his legs began to go, talking low and steady into his ear so that his hands kept working. Lio hung from the gantry rail with a scope, calling out the slow tumble of the wreckage and plotting a lane through it, a narrow corridor of empty dark that opened and closed with the drift. Isa took the half-built hauler, which had no hull to speak of and no flight clearance at all, and flew it on bare thrusters down Lio's lane, easing its blunt nose against the plate until the metal stopped grinding against Mateo's suit. Dov locked the coupling, the crane took the weight, and the plate lifted away.

Mateo came out cracked and bruised and breathing. When the medics cleared him and the yard channel opened for the report, Vance spoke into it before anyone else could. He told it as his rescue: his call to rebuild the crane, his order to commit the hauler, his nerve that had held the line. Dov sat on a cargo crate with his forearms wrapped in gel and said nothing. Isa looked at Lio, and Lio looked at the deck.

Through the gantry's long port, past the drifting glitter of the debris, the lights of Threnody station hung against the black, and a ship lay docked at its outer ring with the rival faction's colors painted down its flank. Vance stood at the glass and studied it. Those people were enemies, he said, and if they got in the way, they would be left behind, the same as any wreckage. He said it in the same voice he had used to promise the crew peace when the charter talks began, and the four of them who had pulled Mateo out of the frame heard both promises at once and could no longer tell which one he meant.

<!-- cell: markdown id="a8675bb4-a964-4d28-8d94-6ffdb4d02ed9" -->

The spoke elevator let them out where the station's spin had thinned to almost nothing, and Teodora had to hook her elbow through a handrail to keep Mateo from drifting off her shoulder. His breath came wet and shallow against her neck. The infirmary hatch stood ten meters along the curve, its white light spilling into the corridor, and between that light and her stood a clerk in the grey-and-copper of the Meridian Shipping Company, a ledger slate held out before him like a collection plate. He did not look at Mateo. He looked at her hands, and then at the line on the slate where her name was meant to go.

"Station policy," he said. "Care is extended against yard wages. Your household's, from the date of signing, until the balance clears." He turned the slate so she could see the columns: her name, her husband's, a third line already filled in for a child too young to hold a welding torch. Teodora had seen that shape of paper before, in her grandmother's kitchen on Earth, with the harvest written where the wages were

<!-- cell: markdown id="d45c266a-5fda-4a17-b8d7-6e28b6c334ee" -->

The infirmary ring turned slowly enough that the water in the drip bags hung almost straight, and Teodora sat on a folding stool beside the third bed with her hands pressed flat between her knees. Mateo's leg lay outside the blanket in a frame of carbon struts, the shin splinted and swollen purple-black from ankle to knee where the cargo clamp had closed on it. He was asleep, or pretending to be. The medic had left a tablet on the tray with the dosage schedule and a line at the bottom she had not wanted to read, and she had turned it face down.

Above the row of beds the screens, which usually cycled the station's air quality and shift rosters, went blue and then showed the crest of the Pacific bloc's intelligence directorate in Santiago. A woman in a gray suit read from a lectern while satellite photographs filled the wall behind her: a launch complex on the equator, its gantries foreshortened from orbit, fuel trucks lined along the service road, and on the pad a squat capture tug in the Andean coalition's white and green. The coalition meant to reach 2041 KX first and take its metals, the woman said, while the lithium field below the salt flats stayed frozen under arbitration. The bloc's leaders would not allow it. Their own tug had been ordered fueled and held on the pad at full readiness, to launch the moment the coalition's lifted. The photographs repeated, closer each time, until the trucks were sharp enough to count.

While she was still speaking, two Meridian Shipping Company technicians in orange coveralls came through the hatch at the far end of the ward with a cart of gray boxes. They worked down the corridor outside without looking at the beds, kneeling at each family's service panel and bolting a meter over the air line and another over the water line, the ratchet clicking in short bursts under the broadcast. When they finished they taped a printed notice beside the hatch. Teodora could read it from her stool. To meet the company's obligations under the bloc launch contract, every lost shift and every course of medical care would be charged against household rations, effective at the start of the next cycle. Beneath it, someone had already scratched the corner with a fingernail, as if testing whether it would come off.

She looked at the meter clamped over the vent above Mateo's bed, its small green numerals already counting. She thought of the dry county outside Lubbock, the dust coming under the door in the mornings like flour, and the lawyer's office in town where she had signed away the last well rights the family owned, the deep one her grandfather had drilled, for enough to buy berths on a Meridian lift. The man had slid the pen across the desk and told her she was buying air. On the screen the woman in gray was saying the bloc's tug would be ready within hours. Teodora reached over and turned the tablet face up, and read the line at the bottom after all.

<!-- cell: markdown id="80125ee6-02c7-4d97-a7a1-1befaeedb185" -->

The Meridian ration office sat on the docking spine like a tollbooth bolted to a bridge, a narrow room of scuffed counters and humming lamps, with the reactor bay's warmth rising through the deck plates underfoot. Teodora waited in the queue with Mateo's chit folded in her fist, a week of his rations docked for reasons no one had written down. When her turn came, the clerk, a thin man with a company badge clipped crooked to his collar, did not argue. He turned his screen toward her and scrolled. Every family on Threnody had a column: so much for the berth, so much for the air they breathed, so much for each liter of ice the company's tugs hauled in from the mining outposts. Mateo's column ran red to the bottom, and so did the one beside it, and the one beside that.

Behind the clerk's shoulder the viewport framed the brown curve of Earth, hazed and dim, and against it the tugs were nosing in to unload, their cargo nets fat with dirty ice that glittered when it turned into the sun. Closer, along the docking ring, figures in company orange moved with cutting torches. They were Vance's people, the loyal hands, stripping the station as he had ordered. Sparks drifted from the struts in slow arcs, and where the ring met the hull beside the reactor bay, panels had already been peeled back to bare frame. Teodora watched a strut sag free and tumble away into the dark, and the clerk did not look up.

Behind her the queue had grown loud. A woman with frost-cracked knuckles was cursing the same ledger, and the ice miners around her took it up: they cut the ice and hauled it, and they were charged by the liter to drink it. Teodora caught the woman's eye and said nothing until the clerk called the next name. Then she touched the woman's sleeve, nodded toward the service hatch at the end of the spine, and slipped through it. One by one, the miners followed.

Below, in a maintenance crawlway that smelled of coolant and old insulation, the others were waiting: Isa with her schematics spread across a crate, Dov and Yuna crouched over a cold lamp, Lio keeping watch at the junction, and the rival engineers who had not shared a shift in years, standing a careful arm's length apart. The miners squeezed in among them. Isa traced the reactor core's housing with one finger, and the plan took shape in low voices. Dov would handle the locks, Yuna the power, the miners the cutting and hauling they knew better than anyone, and the engineers, setting their old grudges aside for an hour, would split the core's restraints between them. Each of them took the task they did best. Above them, through layers of deck and frame, the torches went on cutting into the hull beside the reactor bay, the very place where they meant to work, and none of them heard it.

<!-- cell: markdown id="c2f5cdca-b22e-4558-b5bc-907313d77319" -->

The reclamation deck smelled of chlorine and old pipe, and the tanks rose from the grating like the hulls of buried ships, each one sweating in the cold. Teodora stood on the lip of Tank Four with her elder son beside her, his arm bound to his chest where the gantry clamp had crushed it, and below them the miners and the yard crews filled every aisle between the tanks. Bolted to the wall behind her was a Meridian meter, its green numerals ticking down the family's allotment: so many liters of water and so many breaths, set against wages the yards had stopped paying the day the boy fell. Seven days of ration were gone from the readout. She had pried its faceplate off with a spanner so the crowd could see the wiring. "We left the dust bowls because the wells were bought," she told them, and her voice rang off the steel. "We left the debt because the debt was killing us. Look at this. They have brought the drought up here with us and bolted it to the wall." The crowd answered her in one rough voice, and men began working at the meters along the far bulkhead with pry bars and boot heels.

Above them, in the station's command spindle, Vance sat alone in a squadron office and watched two light-delayed feeds crawl across his console. One fleet had lit its drives toward high orbit, and then the other had lit its drives too. He had written both warnings himself in the careful idiom of each bloc's intelligence service, and each told one side that the other meant to seize the colonies. The resentment that had led him to write them had faded into a cold, attentive patience. On a third screen the colonial supply convoy, nine tankers heavy with ice, drifted along its scheduled lane, and it was about to cross the space where the two fleets would meet. His hand rested near the routing controls. He never touched them.

The flash reached the deck as a flicker on the public screens, then a hush, then a broadcast voice that faltered as it read the tanker names. The fleets had fired on each other, every tanker in the convoy was gone, and the ice that was to have filled these tanks for a season was a glittering cloud of fragments tumbling out of orbit. On the deck a woman let her pry bar fall, and it rang on the grating loud enough for everyone to hear. The meters that still worked went on counting down.

Teodora felt the crowd change under her before anyone spoke. Faces that had been turned up to her now turned toward the screens and the sealed hatches and the gauges on Tank Four, which showed it less than a third full. Someone shouted that the squadron had ships and guns and could hold the station when the blocs came. Someone else shouted Vance's name, and then the shout became a chant, the same voices that had shouted down the meters now pleading for a commander to come and take the station. Her son gripped her sleeve with his good hand. She opened her mouth to call them back, but no one was looking at her anymore. Behind her the meter she had broken open hung by its wires and ticked on.

<!-- cell: markdown id="683842c8-d6f8-45a6-8af1-0000d5a4a410" -->

The crews came down off the hulls of the colony ships in a long, untidy line, clipping their tethers hand over hand along the shipyard spine. Behind them the ships hung half-skinned in their cradles, ribs open to the black, welding arcs going dark one after another as torches were capped and stowed. Someone left a plasma lance drifting at the end of its lanyard, turning slowly against the stars like a compass needle that had lost north. Nobody went back for it.

The survey came in while they were still filing through the spine locks. Every screen on the station carried it at once: the Andean capture tug's spectral sweep of 2041 KX, a rubble-pile asteroid tumbling toward its close pass, the lithium lines bright and thin across the readout. Enough for one bloc's shortage. Not two. Within the hour the Pacific bloc's craft lit its engine in a long, deliberate burn to cut ahead of the tug's approach, and the trajectories on the public plots bent toward each other like drawn blades. Lima and Manila issued their declarations minutes apart, in nearly the same words: any interference with their vessel would be treated as an act of war. The UN's summons to Geneva scrolled underneath both, in a smaller font.

Meridian's answer to the strike arrived through the vents. The air handlers in the strikers' ring sections dropped to a low, laboring hum, and the overhead panels showed the new allotments in cold amber: oxygen partial pressure trimmed, scrubber cycles lengthened, flow rationed by the hour. On the docking boards a shuttle was already logged inbound from the equatorial elevator, its manifest listing forty-two certified hull welders, Earth-hired, contract start on arrival. The company needed its ships finished for the race, and it did not intend to wait for anyone to come back to the cradles.

Teodora stood in the corridor outside Ring Section Four with her palm flat against the wall gauge, as if the cold of it could tell her more than the numbers did. They ticked down in steady, patient decrements. Around her the strikers were talking low, conserving breath without quite knowing they were doing it, and someone's child had fallen asleep against a bulkhead in the middle of the afternoon. She ran the figures the way she would have run a weld schedule. The strike had days, not weeks. Meridian could outlast them simply by doing nothing at all.

But the station breathed on ice. Every gram of oxygen in those vents had come up from the ice miners' haulers, cracked out of comet slush in the processing bays at the far end of the spine, and Meridian answered to them for every breath it handed out or held back. Teodora took her hand from the gauge and looked down the long curve of the corridor toward the mining docks, where the cargo lights were still burning. Without the miners, the strikers would simply run out of air. If the miners stood with them, the air would run short for everyone, Meridian's own people included.

<!-- cell: markdown id="6e010465-e07d-42cd-9e3f-08e93d9a8da8" -->

The ice hauler came in nose-first through the Kessler Belt's glitter of dead satellites, its hull pocked with a thousand small impacts, its belly full of comet ice that steamed faintly where the docking floods touched it. Teodora rode in the cramped cab beside the pilot, and when the clamps took hold and the dock's long throat pressurized, she was the first one out. The miners were already gathering at the unloading rails, gray-faced in the cold, each wrist banded with a Meridian ration meter that ticked down their air and water in amber numerals. She climbed onto a cargo sled and held up her own wrist so they could see the same band, the same numbers. "Every block you unload goes up the line to Halcyon Ring," she said. "It feeds their air. It feeds their water. It feeds the yards. And the yards are already stopped. The shipwrights put down their tools. All that's still moving is you."

The miners argued. Some feared the meters, which Meridian could tighten with a keystroke; some feared the hauler crews behind them in the queue. But an old cutter named Brask stepped to the release lever of the nearest bay, looked at the numbers on his wrist a long moment, and locked it closed. One by one the others followed, until the dock held a row of laden haulers frosting quietly in their cradles, their ice untouched. Far above, on Halcyon Ring, the gauges would begin their slow fall, and the last sounds of work in the shipyards would die away.

In the hush among the idle haulers, Isa spread a salvaged display slate across a crate of drill bits, and her crew crowded in with the rival engineers, who still wore borrowed suits and the wary look of people who weren't yet sure they were welcome. Dov laid his seal schematics over the rivals' scans of the station's reactor, and for the first time the two halves fit: here were the containment seams, there the shielding faults, here the path a crew might take to lift the core free without cracking it open. They argued in low voices about cutting order and coolant bleed, and slowly a plan took shape: a joint extraction, both factions on the lines together, the core's power split down the middle so neither side could starve the other.

Vance, the rivals' lead, leaned close to the slate. "Give me the seal plans," he said. "My people know this reactor. We'll run the first cut." Dov pulled the slate back against his chest. "Both crews, or no crew," he said. "These don't go to one side." Vance said nothing more. Minutes later, while Isa was marking assignments, someone noticed the gap where he had been standing, and the inner hatch to the reactor spine cycling shut behind him.

The shout went down the dock like a pressure alarm. Isa was moving before anyone had finished saying his name, Dov at her heels with the slate. The rival engineers came after them, and the strikers came after them, and then the ice miners, Brask among them with a cutting bar still in his hand. They all poured toward the reactor hatch, their breath fogging in the cold, the ration meters on every wrist still ticking down together.

<!-- cell: markdown id="8ff893df-cb8c-487b-8e68-7afd595aa038" -->

The central concourse of Halcyon Ring curved upward at both ends until its far reaches hung overhead like a ceiling of other people. Beneath the Meridian executive spindle, where the deck widened into a plaza of scuffed composite and dead fountains, the strikers had packed themselves shoulder to shoulder. They wore their shift coveralls with the sleeves knotted at the waist, and many carried the small grey rebreathers the infirmary had stopped issuing. The air tasted of metal and old breath. Somewhere in the ducts a fan labored and caught and labored again, and every time it faltered a ripple of silence passed through the crowd. Through the long viewport behind the spindle's base, the ice haulers drifted at their moorings, fat with frozen water, their cargo locks sealed.

Vance came down to the mezzanine rail without guards. He was a narrow man in a pressed grey jacket, and he stood with his hands folded on the rail as if he were about to hear a confession. A woman near the front, her hair cropped short for the rebreather strap, raised her voice first: unload the haulers, restore the ration, send word to the Council that the station would deal, and the Council's ships could turn back. Others took it up. A man held a child on his hip and simply pointed at her, at her cracked lips, and said nothing at all. Vance let them finish. Then he told them, in a voice that carried without strain, that the Council's ships were not coming to save anyone, that Earth had written them off the day it stopped answering their freight bids, and that the only hands between them and the vacuum were his. He said it gently, and he did not lower his eyes.

When the shouting rose again he turned and went back up the spindle, and the lift doors closed on him. In the operations gallery three decks above, he stood before the distribution board and moved the last of the stored ice, the reserve that had sat in the cold tanks since the haulers stopped, into two channels. One fed the executive decks. The other ran out along the spine to the dry dock, where the colony ship hung in its scaffold, half-skinned, its long hull meant one day to carry families up from the parched cities below. The technician at the board watched the gauges shift and did not speak.

Then Vance leaned toward her and asked, quietly, for the strikers' ring. Not the whole ring, he said. Cut the meters by section, at random, a few at a time. Leave some taps running. She keyed it in. Down on the concourse, the first word spread from someone whose wrist unit had just gone red: their section was dry, the next section over was not. Heads turned. People who had stood together all morning began to look at one another's rebreathers, at one another's bottles, at the doors to one another's quarters. The woman with the cropped hair was still shouting up at the empty mezzanine, but fewer voices were answering her, and the crowd had begun, very slowly, to come apart into its sections.

<!-- cell: markdown id="af1a3fa9-0f65-4b3a-a29a-0dafbe3f56b7" -->

The shuttle lock opened onto the arrivals deck of Halcyon Ring with a sigh of equalizing pressure, and the passengers spilled out in a slow crush of duffels and elbows. Teodora Vass came through with a hand on each of her sons' shoulders, the older one already taller than she was and pretending not to need the hand, the younger one craning his neck at the ceiling where the station's spin made the far end of the deck curve up and out of sight. The air smelled of machine oil and recycled breath and, underneath it, something she had not smelled in three years: wet metal. Condensation beaded on the coolant lines overhead and ran down in thin, careless streaks. She watched a drop fall and break on the deck plating and nobody moved to catch it. The farm had gone for less than the land was worth and a little more than three tickets up, and the brochure had promised shipyard work and water that never ran out. Here, at least, was the water.

Past the customs gate the crowd thinned, and at the edge of the deck, beside a salvage tug docked with its hatch hanging open, a handful of people stood in a loose circle under a flickering work lamp. A lean man in a patched pressure jacket was talking to them in a low, steady voice, and the arrivals line slowed as people turned to look. Teodora heard the name Threnody, then the name Aurel Vance spoken by a woman beside her who said it the way people on the surface had once said the names of rainmakers. Vance had a star chart open on a slate, a dead station glowing at its center like a coal. The reactor there still held its charge, he said. Enough power to run every hab in the Belt for a generation. Enough that no faction would have to fight another for a single kilowatt again. The war would end because there would be nothing left to fight over.

The pilot, a broad-shouldered woman with flight tape around her knuckles, raised her hand first. "Morrow's in," someone muttered, and Isa Morrow did not lower her hand until Vance met her eyes and nodded. The engineer beside her, a gray-bearded man with a burn scar running up one forearm, studied the chart a long moment, then said, "Petrak too. If it's real, I want to be the one who touches it." The medic was smaller than the others and had a field kit slung across her chest; Yuna Sato looked at each face in the circle before she lifted her hand, slowly, as if setting it down on a table. The navigator, Lio Banks, was the youngest of them, barely older than Teodora's eldest, and he laughed when he raised his hand, a short, nervous sound, and said that someone had to plot the course or they'd all end up as part of the Belt.

Four hands stood in the lamplight. Vance closed the slate and said nothing for a moment, and in that silence Teodora's younger son tugged her sleeve and asked whether those people were going to stop the war. She did not answer. She watched the crew file toward the open hatch of the tug, Morrow first, Banks last, his boots ringing on the gangway, and she watched Vance stand alone under the work lamp after they had gone in, looking down at his hands. Then the arrivals line pressed forward behind her, and a station clerk called for papers, and the water went on dripping from the coolant lines overhead, falling and breaking and falling again.

<!-- cell: markdown id="c431392b-e877-4b89-a45f-a427589915d3" -->

The hiring hall of Halcyon Ring smelled of welding flux and too many bodies breathing the same air. Teodora stood at the clerk's window with the family's arrival chit in her hand and read the figures twice before she understood them. The passage up had not been paid in full. The berth they had slept in for nine nights was billed by the shift, and every liter of water drawn from the recyclers had been metered against their name, down to the decimal. The clerk, a thin man with a cough he did not bother to cover, turned the screen toward her so she could see the total glowing amber. Behind her the line shuffled forward and someone muttered about people who held things up.

Mateo did not argue. He walked to the yard board where the contracts scrolled in white text and pressed his thumb to the first one that paid by the hull: ore haulers, blunt-nosed cargo frames for the new mining outposts out among the rocks, steady work for a man who could hold a seam straight in vacuum. When the foreman asked which rotation he wanted, Mateo asked for two. The foreman looked at him a moment, then shrugged and stamped both. Teodora watched her husband sign his name to sixteen hours a day and said nothing, because there was nothing to say that the amber numbers had not already said.

All around them the hall hummed with another kind of talk. Men waiting at the board spoke low and leaned close. A surface shipping company, one of the big planetside lines, wanted the station's charter; someone's cousin in the dock office had seen the lawyers come up on the last shuttle; the Ring would be sold and everyone's contracts would be torn up or rewritten. Nobody knew which company, and nobody knew whether it was true. That did not stop it from passing from mouth to mouth until the whole hall seemed to be saying it at once.

Through the wall, in the records vault, the air was cold and still and the only light came from the terminals. Vance sat alone at the end of a long row of them, his jaw set as it had been since Geneva, and he could hear the murmur of the hall as a faint vibration in the bulkhead. Frightened people, he thought. People already counting what they owed and wondering who would protect them. Give them a reason to believe the rival blocs were about to go to war, and they would come asking for a strong hand on the station, and he meant to be that hand. But a war scare needed signals, and signals needed the ciphers, and the ciphers were held by one officer. He opened the squadron personnel files and the station's own security records side by side and began to read, file after file, cross-checking dates and seals, until a single line stopped him. Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran's clearance history carried an entry that did not match the originating office's log. The stamp was right, the signature was right, and the date was impossible. Someone had forged it, and the deletion trail showed that she had buried it herself. Vance copied the record to a private chip, closed the files one by one, and sat back in the cold while the hall beyond the wall went on whispering about the charter.

<!-- cell: markdown id="f293e647-1541-46a1-84ca-d1d9f9249037" -->

The council chamber of the Halcyon Ring sat at the station's slow outer rim, where the spin gave a weight almost honest enough to stand on. Through the long window behind the councillors' table the Earth turned blue and white, and somewhere under that cloud lay the lithium field that neither army could touch now. The Geneva freeze had been signed as an armistice, and its text still glowed on the chamber's wall screen, thirty clauses ending in two flags. Nobody in the room looked at it. They were looking at the two agents of the Meridian Shipping Company, who had come up on the morning shuttle in grey suits that had never been worn in vacuum.

The elder agent spoke without notes. The Pacific bloc, she said, had launched a prospecting probe from its leased pad at Kourou toward the asteroid 2041 KX, a dark lump of metal tumbling well beyond the Moon, and had publicly staked a survey claim on its nickel and its lithium. The claim named the Halcyon Ring as its base of operations. Meridian would carry the ore if the station could show it was worth the freight. She let that sit, folded her hands, and asked when the hauler would be ready.

The councillors conferred in low voices behind cupped palms. Then the chair announced that the ore hauler's delivery date would be moved up by eleven weeks, and that the gantry safety crews would be reduced to meet the new schedule. At the back of the chamber, among the workers' representatives who stood because there were not enough seats, Mateo Quispe kept his hands in the pockets of his patched coverall. Everyone on the gantry rosters knew what the order meant without hearing it read: the families who owed the station for their passage up would work double shifts, longer ones, on scaffolds with no spotter and no one watching the tether clips, and any family that refused would lose its berth and its water ration at the end of the month. The clerk entered the vote. Nobody from the rosters was asked to speak.

In the gallery above, the three observers of the Andean coalition had followed all of it on their slates. When the chair invited comment, the eldest of them rose and spoke into the chamber microphone in a dry, careful voice. Their rival, she said, had lost the battery metals on the ground at Geneva, and it meant to win them back in orbit, one asteroid at a time, from stations exactly like this one. She sat down. The Meridian agents smiled politely, the Earth turned past the window, and Mateo walked out toward the spokeway lift with his shift beginning in forty minutes.

<!-- cell: markdown id="04e92b2c-2f0a-4350-870d-aed0518aa288" -->

The announcement came over the gantry channels at the start of second shift, in a voice too smooth for the yards. The Meridian Shipping Company now held the charter of Halcyon Ring. From the next cycle, every family's air and water would be drawn against yard wages. The welders on the long spars heard it through their helmets and kept welding, because the ore haulers for the asteroid outposts were already three days late. Mateo, Teodora's elder son, was eleven hours into a double shift on the keel of the fourth hauler when the Kessler Belt sent its answer through the yard: a spray of old wreckage, bolts and panel shards and the tumbling half of some forgotten satellite, moving faster than anything could dodge.

The strike sheared a crane coupling, and the hull plate it had been holding swung loose on one cable. It caught Mateo against the keel truss and stayed there, grinding, while his suit alarms climbed. Vance's voice filled every channel at once, telling them to move, to hurry, to get him out, telling no one how. Dov went to the coupling with his torch. A ruptured line flashed across his forearm and burned through two layers of his suit, and he went on working with the sealant foam hardening over the wound. Yuna braced herself against the spar beside him, one hand clamped on his harness, talking low and steady each time his knees went slack. Out past the gantries, Lio hung at the edge of the drift with his scope and called numbers, picking out a lane through the slow-turning debris where nothing would cross for ninety seconds.

Isa took the lane. The hauler had no cockpit fitted and no hull past its midsection, only a thruster block and a bare frame, and she flew it on attitude jets by eye, nudging the open bones of it up beneath the swinging plate until frame met steel and held. Dov's rebuilt coupling bit. The cable drew taut. The plate lifted a hand's width off the truss, then an arm's length, and Yuna let go of Dov long enough to drag Mateo out by his tether. His visor was cracked and his left leg hung at the wrong angle, but he was breathing, and he swore at all of them in a voice that was plainly his own.

Vance was the first to the airlock when they brought Mateo in, and the first to speak to the yard supervisors waiting there. He told it well. He had kept his head, he said. He had directed the crane repair, ordered the hauler into position, found the path through the wreckage. Dov stood behind him with his arm wrapped in a field dressing and said nothing. Isa looked at the deck. Lio opened his mouth, and Yuna touched his sleeve, and he closed it again.

Later they gathered at the observation port above the yard, and Lio was the one who spotted the ship. It was docked at Threnody, the smaller station hanging a few hundred kilometers off, its rival markings plain under magnification. Vance leaned close to the glass. Those people were enemies, he said, and if they got in the way, they would be left behind, simple as that. Nobody answered him. For weeks he had spoken of peace, of reaching terms with Threnody once the yards were secured, and the crew had believed him the way people believe a promise they need. Now Isa watched his reflection in the port. Dov flexed his burned hand. Yuna's eyes went from Vance to the distant ship and back, and none of them looked at the others, because each was afraid of finding the same doubt there.

<!-- cell: markdown id="f218a5fa-5256-4ada-adde-c213a593dea2" -->

The spoke elevator rose toward the hub, and with every deck it passed Mateo grew lighter in Teodora's arms. By the time the doors parted on the infirmary ring she could hold him against her hip with one arm, his head lolling on her shoulder, the blood on his sleeve gone tacky and brown. The hatch stood twenty steps away, its white light spilling across the deck plates. Between her and that light stood a clerk in the grey tunic of the Meridian Shipping Company, a slate held out before him like a tray.

"Care is extended against the ledger," he said, and he did not look at the boy. "Name, berth, employer of record. Then the pledge." The slate showed a column of figures already filled in: the yard wages she had not yet earned, and her husband's, and Mateo's own when he came of age, each line carried forward against a rate of interest set in small type at the bottom. Teodora had seen such a page before, on Earth, in her father's kitchen, when the company man came at planting time and the harvest that had not yet grown was signed over against seed and water. Her hand knew the motion. She shifted Mateo higher, took the stylus, and wrote her name where the clerk's finger rested. Only then did the man step aside.

A few paces from the hatch, Vance leaned against the bulkhead with his sleeve pushed back, reading his wrist slate as if he were waiting for an appointment. The traffic scrolling there belonged to neither him nor the station: Coalition fleet orders and Assembly logistics, both blocs' messages laid open by cipher keys that should never have left their vaults. One line held his eye. A colonial supply convoy, nine hulls of grain and machine parts and families bound outward, was routed through the disputed lane in four days' time. He read it twice. Then he marked the lane on his slate as the place where the scare would happen. A few unclaimed contacts on the scopes, a burst of false fire, and the station would beg for a protector. He would answer that call, and the Council, frightened and grateful, would give him command before anyone asked where the danger had come from.

Mara had been sent to the infirmary ring to fetch the convoy's course and plot it against the lane charts, and she stood near the elevator with that order glowing on her own slate. She had watched the woman sign. She had watched the clerk take the stylus back and wipe it on his sleeve, and she had watched the boy carried through into the white light. Now her eyes moved from the convoy's projected track to the shaded band of the disputed lane, and to Vance, who was smiling slightly at something on his wrist. The lines met. Nine ships of colonists would be passing through that band exactly when the guns began, and none of them would know the fight was staged, and the guns would not know it either.

<!-- cell: markdown id="68db4e0d-6294-4371-a23c-ebec3becce34" -->

The infirmary ring turned slowly enough that a cup of water leaned only a little in its holder, and Teodora had been watching it lean for some time. Mateo lay on the cot beside her with his left leg cased in a gray splint from hip to ankle, the shin beneath it flattened and purpled where the bone had given way. The sedative had loosened his face without quite closing his eyes. She kept one hand on his wrist, counting the pulse without meaning to, while the screens set into the curved ceiling glowed the blue of an official channel.

The bulletin came from Santiago, from the intelligence directorate of the Pacific bloc, and it was read by a woman in a pressed collar who did not blink. Satellite photographs filled the panel behind her: a launch complex on the equator, a long white tug standing in its gantry, fuel trucks drawn up around its base like beetles at a carcass. The Andean coalition, the woman said, was preparing a capture mission to 2041 KX. Since the lithium field remained frozen and no ruling on it was expected, the bloc's leadership would not allow its rival to take the asteroid's metals unopposed. A bloc tug had been ordered fueled and held on its own pad, cleared to launch the moment the coalition's did. The photographs cycled again, the same white tug from a slightly different angle, and the woman began the bulletin over from the top.

Out in the corridor a drill shrieked against steel. Teodora turned and saw two crewmen in the orange coveralls of the Meridian Shipping Company working their way along the wall, one holding a boxy meter against the air main while the other ran bolts through its flanges. When they finished they moved to the water line and did the same, then to the next family's hatch, and the next, the drill whining every few seconds, nobody stopping to explain. Their supervisor carried a tablet and kept glancing at it, and Teodora heard him tell them the bloc wanted its launch contract covered before the end of the week. As they passed the infirmary door one of them slapped a printed notice onto the frame and smoothed it flat with his glove. Lost shifts and medical care, it read, would be charged against household rations, effective immediately.

Teodora read the notice twice, then looked down at the splint, at the metered line hissing faintly behind the wall. She remembered the last well, the one on the north edge of the property where the dust had drifted against the casing in long tan ridges, and the man from the water consortium who had walked the boundary with a handheld survey unit and never once looked at the house. She remembered signing the transfer on the hood of his truck because there was no table left that was not sold, and holding the berth tickets afterward, three of them, warm from the printer. Above her the woman in the pressed collar finished speaking, and the white tug stood in its gantry again, waiting.

<!-- cell: markdown id="80d3b3f5-9d0d-4416-95ab-833ebd8e5b7e" -->

The Meridian ration office sat on the docking spine like a tick on a vein, a narrow room of scuffed bulkheads and humming lamps, and the queue that morning ran out the hatch and down the corridor toward the reactor bay. Teodora waited in it with Mateo's chit folded in her fist until the paper went soft. When she reached the counter she laid it flat and pressed her thumb on the stamp that said DOCKED, SEVEN DAYS, as though she could rub the ink away. The clerk, a gray young man with a company badge clipped crooked on his collar, did not argue. He turned his screen toward her and scrolled. There was the ledger: every family on Threnody listed by berth, and against each name the charges ran in tidy columns. Berth rent. Air, by the cubic meter. Water, by the liter, priced against the ice the company's tugs hauled in from the mining outposts. Mateo's line ended in red. "It isn't me," the clerk said quietly. "It's the arithmetic."

Behind him a long viewport looked out along the spine. The tugs were coming in, blunt and scarred, nosing up to the unloading cradles with their cargo nets swollen with dirty ice, and beyond them hung the Earth, a brown curve hazed at the edge, too large and too close to look at for long. Teodora looked anyway. Below the cradles, near the docking ring, small suited figures moved along the station's skin. Vance's men, the ones who wore his colors on their sleeves, were working with cutting torches, and the white sparks spat and died in the vacuum as strut after strut came free and drifted. They worked steadily, without hurry, down toward the plating that shielded the reactor bay, and where they passed, the hull's bracing looked thin as a ribcage.

Behind her the queue had begun to mutter. Three ice miners, still in frost-stained coveralls from the tugs, were reading their own chits aloud to one another and swearing. One of them had worked twenty hours cutting ice on an outpost rock to come home to half rations; another laughed without humor and said the air he'd breathed on the way in had cost more than he'd hauled. Teodora took back Mateo's chit, slid it into her sleeve, and turned. She did not raise her voice. She caught the eye of the nearest miner, then the next, and tipped her head toward a maintenance hatch at the end of the corridor, and when she slipped through it they followed, one at a time, a few minutes apart, so that no one at the counter marked them going.

The ladder dropped three decks into the warm dark beside the reactor bay, where the pipes sweated and the deck plates thrummed underfoot. Isa was waiting there with a lamp, and Dov beside her with his arms folded, and Yuna crouched over a schematic chalked straight onto the bulkhead, and Lio pacing. Across the narrow space stood the rival engineers, the ones who had never once shared a shift with Isa's crew, wary and silent. No one wasted time on that now. Yuna traced the coolant lines; Dov claimed the locks, because no one opened a sealed door faster; Lio took the watch; the miners, who knew cold and pressure better than anyone aboard, said they would handle the venting. The rival engineers took the core housing itself, and Isa took the timing. They leaned in over the chalk lines, close enough to hear one another breathe, building their plan to free the reactor core inch by inch. On the other side of the bulkhead they leaned against, out in the hard light above the brown Earth, the torches kept on cutting.

<!-- cell: markdown id="5dfb587b-10db-468d-8b30-fc0da778b2ef" -->

The reclamation deck smelled of chlorine and old pipe, and the great tanks stood in rows under the work lamps like grain silos turned to steel. Teodora climbed onto a valve housing so the back of the crowd could see her. Miners still grey with regolith dust stood shoulder to shoulder with yard crews in their scorched coveralls, and between them the children had been shushed into quiet. On the bulkhead behind her glowed the Meridian Shipping Company's new meter panel, a column of green figures for each family: litres of water, cubic metres of air, each set against a wage. Her elder son's line had gone amber. A torn shoulder from a falling hull plate, a week off the yards, a week's ration docked. He stood below her with his arm strapped to his chest and would not look at the numbers.

"We left the dust bowls for this," she said, and her voice rang off the tanks. "We left the wells that went dry and the banks that took the farms. We came up here to breathe without asking a man's permission." She pointed at the panel. "They have hung the same drought on that wall. They have written the same debt in green. We do not take another reading. Not one family. If they want to shut off the air, let them shut it off on all of us at once, and let them explain it to the Council." The crowd answered her with a low rolling sound, and somebody took a spanner to the nearest meter housing and the plastic cracked like ice.

Far beyond the station's hull, Commodore Vance's squadron held its position and kept silent. The Council had passed him over before the whole assembly, and he had answered with two dispatches of his own making, one to each of the rival blocs, each swearing that the other meant to seize the orbital colonies. Now both fleets were burning hard for high orbit. Their courses closed on the lane where the colonial supply convoy crawled toward Halcyon with its belly full of ice. Vance watched the plot. He had the guns to cover the convoy and the time to warn it, and he did neither. When the first salvos crossed, the water haulers burst one after another, and their cargo spread into a glittering cloud that no one would ever drink.

The word came down to the tanks by handset and by running boy, and the noise of the crowd changed. Teodora heard it happen: the rough unity broke into separate voices, mothers counting days out loud, a miner asking how deep the reserve tanks went and getting no answer anyone wanted. The cracked meter hung forgotten on its bracket. Someone shouted Vance's name, and then others took it up, not as a curse but as a plea. *His ships are right there. He has the guns. Let him take the station. Let him keep the fleets off us.* Teodora stood on the valve housing with her hand still raised, and below her the crowd that had come to refuse a master turned its faces toward the viewports and begged one to come.

<!-- cell: markdown id="695c81dd-80bf-471a-92a7-dfcbe562d298" -->

The yard crews came up the shipyard spine still smelling of the reclamation tanks, where the vote had been taken over the hum of the algae pumps, and one by one they set down their torches. Along the half-built hulls of the colony ships the scaffolding lights stayed on, white and useless, throwing the shadows of empty harnesses across plating that would not be seamed today. Nobody shouted. A rigger named Osei clipped his tether to the rail, coiled the slack, and hung his welding mask on a stanchion like a man leaving a coat at the door. Behind him the others did the same, until the spine was lined with faceless masks staring out at the ships.

The survey came in while they were still filing past. It played on every public slate along the spine at once: the Andean capture tug's spectral sweep of 2041 KX, a slow tumble of rock rendered in false color, its lithium bands glowing a thin and stingy green. The analysts' figures crawled beneath the image. There was enough for one bloc's shortfall. Not both. Within the hour the station's feeds showed the Pacific craft lighting its drive, a clean blue line bending toward the asteroid on a burn meant to cut ahead, and then the two governments, one after the other, using nearly the same words: any interference would be considered an act of war. A third notice followed, flat and procedural, from the United Nations. Both delegations were summoned to Geneva.

Meridian's answer to the strike arrived without a statement. In the ring sections where the strikers bunked, the air handlers dropped to a lower register. The change was small enough to miss, a softening of the breeze from the ceiling vents, but the meters at every hatch began to show it. Oxygen partial pressure ticked down by tenths. Carbon dioxide crept up. At the spine's Earth-side dock a shuttle was already being cleared for berthing, its manifest posted for anyone to read: forty-two contract welders, certified, inbound.

Teodora stood at the hatch of Section Nine with her palm flat against the cold frame and watched the numbers move. She did the arithmetic twice, the way she had been taught to check a weld, and it came out the same both times. The strikers could hold their breath against Meridian for days, maybe a week, but not longer than the air would last, and the air was not Meridian's to give. Every breath on the Halcyon Ring started as ice, cracked and hauled in from the outer belt by the miners whose freighters docked on the far side of the station. Their cargo filled the electrolysis tanks and became the oxygen in the vents, and Meridian throttled it, and it would go on flowing to Meridian for exactly as long as the miners kept hauling it. She looked across the curve of the ring toward their docks, a distant cluster of running lights, and understood that the yard crews could not win this alone.

<!-- cell: markdown id="877c617c-cf3a-446b-9eec-55a093144f0e" -->

The hauler *Brine Saint* came into Threnody's dock nose-first through a curtain of glittering debris, its ice tanks frosted white and groaning against their straps. Teodora rode it in standing in the open cargo lock, one glove hooked through a tether ring, and she was already talking before the clamps finished biting. The miners drifted up to meet her in their scuffed orange suits, and above every berth the Meridian meters glowed green, counting out each breath of air and each liter of water against the next shift's pay. She pointed at them. Every hauler in that dock carried enough ice to keep Halcyon Ring breathing for a week, she said, and every miner who brought it in went home to the same rationed air the shipyard welders did. The yards were already on strike. Without ice, the Ring would have to bargain. One by one the miners let go of their unloading lines. The cargo arms folded back against the bulkheads, the tanks stayed sealed, and on the dock's freight board the outbound schedule to Halcyon blinked from green to amber to a long, unbroken red.

The haulers hung idle in their cradles, and in the shadow between two of them Isa's crew cleared a cargo pallet and spread out the work. Dov unrolled his seal schematics, hand-annotated and soft at the creases. Across from him the rival engineers, their suits still patched with emergency foam, projected their reactor scans into the cold air above the pallet. For the first time the two halves fit. The scans showed where the core's housing had fractured, and Dov's drawings showed how the seals could hold the core while it was drawn out of it. Isa traced a route with one finger from the dock to the reactor shaft, and a rival engineer named Oyelaran traced the return. They argued over cable runs and coupling loads, and in the end they agreed to split the output down the middle: half the core's power for each faction, metered by nobody.

Vance had said little. When the plan was set, he held out his hand for the schematics so that he could study the seal sequence himself, alone and overnight. Dov drew the pages back against his chest and shook his head. The plans stayed on the pallet where everyone could see them, he said, or they stayed with him. Vance smiled, shrugged, and pushed off toward the rear of the dock as though he were looking for a ration line.

A miner was the first to notice the airlock to the reactor spine cycling with only one suit inside it. She shouted it across the dock, and the work fell apart. Isa was already moving, Dov behind her with the schematics stuffed into his chest pouch, and the rival engineers kicked off the pallet after them. Miners abandoned their idle haulers and strikers left their picket at the freight board, and the whole crowd streamed up the spine of Threnody toward the reactor, hand over hand along the guide rails, chasing the single small light that had gone ahead of them into the dark.

<!-- cell: markdown id="e760669c-4132-4621-a44f-4c413c66e934" -->

The concourse beneath the Meridian spindle had been built to hold a festival crowd, and that morning it held more than that. Strikers from the outer rings stood shoulder to shoulder under the long curve of the skylight, where the ice haulers hung docked and dark against the stars, their holds still sealed, frost blooming on their flanks. The air tasted of tin and old breath. Children sat on their parents' shoulders to reach the thinner, cooler layer near the ceiling vents, and a woman near the front held up an empty water bladder, pinched flat, as though it were a petition in itself. When Vance stepped out onto the glass gallery above them, the noise did not rise the way it would have a week earlier. People were saving their lungs. The voices that reached him were hoarse and almost polite: unload the haulers, restore the rations, call the Council's ships back before someone fired on them.

Vance let them finish. He was a narrow, silver-haired man in a pressed grey coat, and he stood with his hands folded on the rail as if he were listening to a quarterly report. When he answered, the gallery speakers carried his voice down without strain. Earth, he told them, had stopped answering for Halcyon long before the strike. The Council's ships were not coming to rescue anyone; they were coming to collect. There was no one left down that gravity well who would spend a liter of water on people this far from home. "I am the reason you are still breathing," he said, almost gently. "I am the only reason you will keep breathing. Remember that when you go home tonight."

Behind the gallery glass, in the spindle's operations room, his orders had already gone out. The last reserve ice, the cold blue cores held back in the station's deep tanks against exactly this kind of emergency, was rerouted up the spine to the executive decks and on through the long transfer line to the berth where the colony ship *Covenant* sat half-plated in her scaffolding. She was meant to carry families off a planet gone to dust and wildfire, and her tanks drank the station's water without a sound. A junior engineer, sitting where the strikers below could not see him, read the next instruction twice before he entered it. Vance had asked for nothing dramatic. No lockdowns, no security sweeps. Only that the air and water meters on Ring Four, the strikers' ring, be switched off, so that no household could see what it was drawing or what its neighbors were.

By the third shift the effect was already plain in the corridors of Ring Four. Without the meters, every hiss from a neighbor's vent sounded like theft, and every running tap behind a closed hatch sounded like a betrayal. Doors that had stood open for weeks of shared kettles and shared grievances were sealed and stayed that way. Men who had linked arms on the concourse that morning argued in the stairwells over whose children were breathing more than their share, and the word "Meridian" was spoken less often than the names of the families on the next deck. When the foremen came down the spokes two days later with work rosters and promises of rations for anyone who signed, the line formed before they had finished unrolling the lists. Up in the spindle, Vance read the count of signatures without comment, and asked to be told when the *Covenant*'s tanks were full.

<!-- cell: markdown id="f8fd3d24-6007-46bb-998a-3fbea7c092df" -->

The asteroid came up over the rim of the habitat ring the way weather comes up over mountains, slow and enormous, filling the long windows a hand's width at a time. 2041 KX was a scarred lump of nickel and iron the color of old pans, and two ships clung to it like ticks: on its sunward face the Andean tug, squat and red-striped, its grapples sunk deep into the regolith; on its shadowed face the Pacific bloc craft, white and needle-nosed, holding just as hard. Neither had let go in all the long haul up from the near-Earth lanes. Their governments' orders still scrolled across every public screen on Halcyon Ring, side by side and word for word the same: the station's shipyards would build outposts on the rock, and they would build them for one flag only. The yards had built nothing. For weeks the slip cradles had hung empty along the spindle, the welding arms folded, and the ice haulers from the mining outposts sat docked at the hub with full holds and sealed hatches, because no one would unload them.

The air began to thin in the eighth hour of the ring's night. It started as a pressure in the ears, then a dry, tight ache behind the eyes, and then the panel lights in every corridor turned amber and a calm recorded voice announced that Meridian had reduced atmospheric supply to Habitat Ring C *pending restoration of contracted labor*. Children woke crying with nosebleeds. Old miners who had grown up in the drought towns, where the surface companies had once shut off the water mains street by street until the pickets broke, sat up in their bunks and knew exactly what was happening. One of them laughed, a short dry sound with no breath behind it. "Same valve," he said. "Different pipe."

Teodora was already in the common hall when the crews began to gather, a cutting torch hooked to her belt and a coil of tether over one shoulder. She did not make a speech. She pointed up at the hub, where the four great spokes met the shipyard control spindle, and said that the air and water valves for the whole station sat in that spindle, and that Meridian's people sat beside them, and that a ring could not be suffocated by people it had to look in the face. Then she turned and went to the nearest spoke hatch, and the miners and the yard crews went after her: drillers in patched vacuum suits, welders with their visors pushed up, ice handlers carrying pry bars, a few hundred of them breathing short and fast in the thinning air.

They climbed. Weight bled away rung by rung as the spoke carried them inward, until they were hauling themselves hand over hand, and then simply pushing off and floating in a long ragged column toward the hub. Through the spoke's narrow ports the asteroid hung directly overhead now, vast and pitted and very close, the two ships still locked to it like dogs on a single bone. It turned slowly against the stars, indifferent to the flags that claimed it and to the people climbing beneath it. At the top of the spoke the spindle's pressure door showed red. Teodora braced her boots against the frame, lit the torch, and set its blue tongue against the seal.

<!-- cell: markdown id="3c769ab3-cbc0-4771-b7ad-ec3486436d0a" -->

The shuttle's ramp unfolded onto the arrivals deck of Halcyon Ring with a hiss of equalizing air, and the crowd pressed out before it had finished settling. Teodora Vass came down in the middle of it with a duffel strap biting her shoulder and a hand on each of her sons: Mattei, twelve and pretending not to need it, and little Pavo, who had his face turned up at the curve of the deck where the floor rose into a wall and the wall became a ceiling. Red dust from the surface still sat in the seams of their boots. In her coat pocket the bill of sale for forty hectares of cracked ground had gone soft from handling. Above the customs gates a banner promised SHIPYARD WORK — ALL SKILLS — and beneath it a public fountain ran in a clear unbroken sheet. Pavo pulled free and put both hands into it, and Teodora did not stop him.

Past the fountain, near a bank of cargo lifts, a knot of people in patched pressure suits stood apart from the arrivals traffic. At its center a lean man in a salvager's jacket held up a data slate showing a dark wheel of a station against the debris glitter of the Belt, its name stenciled across the image in faded block letters: THRENODY. "The reactor's intact," he was saying, loud enough that heads in the customs line turned. "Cold, but intact. Whoever lights it controls the power for half the Belt, and nobody has to fight over scraps of it anymore. Not the Ring, not the Spokes, not the Free Haulers. It ends the war. I'm not asking you to believe me. I'm asking for a vote."

A broad-shouldered woman with pilot's wings worn bare on her collar raised her hand first. "Isa Morrow. I'll fly it." Beside her a bearded man with grease worked into the creases of his knuckles grunted and lifted his. "Petrak. If the core's sound, I'll make it sing." A small woman with a medic's satchel across her chest studied the slate a moment longer than the others before her hand went up. "Sato," she said quietly. "Someone should be there to patch you all up." The last of them, a young navigator with star charts inked down one forearm, grinned and raised both hands. "Banks. You need someone who can find the place." The man with the slate lowered it and looked at each of them in turn. "Then it's unanimous," Aurel Vance said. "We leave on the next window."

Teodora had stopped walking without meaning to. Mattei tugged her sleeve, staring at the four raised hands and the dark wheel on the slate, and asked what war they meant. Around them the arrivals crowd broke and flowed past toward the customs gates. The fountain kept running, Pavo's wet hands dripping onto the deck plating, and the hiring banner glowed over all of it in the station's steady, sourceless light.

<!-- cell: markdown id="c77571fd-8ad9-411e-ab74-9af8b958e28e" -->

The shuttle hatch cycled open with a hiss of equalizing air, and the passengers spilled onto the arrivals deck of Halcyon Ring all at once, elbows and bundles and children lifted over heads. Teodora Vass came down the ramp with a duffel on each shoulder and a son in each hand. The younger, Pavel, stopped dead at the bottom and pointed past the customs rail, where a column of clear water fell three stories from the ceiling into a basin and was pumped back up to fall again. "Is that it, Mama? Is that the water?" "That's it," Teodora said. "It never runs out. Not in the dry months, not ever." Mihai, the elder, hitched his pack higher and did not look at the fountain. "You said the well would never run out." "The well was ours," she said, "and now it's someone else's, and it paid for the seats you just sat in. So keep hold of your brother and don't lose anything else."

They shuffled forward with the crowd while a station voice read berth numbers overhead. Mihai asked where the shipyards were, and Teodora pulled a creased recruitment chit from her coat and held it up to the light as if it might have changed during the climb. "Ring Seven. They hire at the next shift bell. Welders, haulers, anyone with two hands and a stomach for vacuum." "I've got two hands," Pavel said. "You've got two hands and no sense," said Mihai, but he took the chit from her and read it twice. Near the cargo gates the crowd thickened and stalled around a knot of people in patched salvage coveralls, and the three of them were pressed to its edge.

A broad, gray-bearded man stood on a cargo crate in the middle of them, and he spoke without raising his voice, as if he expected people to lean in, and they did. "Threnody has been dark for eleven years," Aurel Vance said. "Everyone in the Belt thinks her reactor cracked when they abandoned her. It didn't. I've seen the readings." A thick-shouldered man with grease to the wrists folded his arms. "Readings off a dead station, Aurel. You know what that's worth." "I know what it's worth if they're right, Dov," Aurel said. "Enough power for every habitat from here to the Vesta lanes. Nobody fights over water and air when there's enough to go around. I don't mean to take Threnody for one faction. I mean to give her to all of them at once, so none of them has a reason left to shoot." Dov Petrak only grunted. "And if the core's slag?" "Then we've burned fuel for nothing, and you can tell me so all the way home."

"Hands, then," said a lean woman with a pilot's wings stitched crooked on her sleeve, and she raised her own first. "I'll fly it. I've flown enough bodies back from the lanes." A small woman with a medkit slung at her hip lifted hers beside it. "Somebody has to patch you when it goes wrong, Isa." "When," Isa Morrow said. "Thank you, Yuna." A young man at the back held up a slate instead of his hand, glowing with a plotted course. "I may have already done the burn calculations," Lio Banks admitted, and someone laughed. Dov looked at each of them in turn, then at Aurel, and finally put his hand up slowly, as though it weighed more than the rest. "Four," he said. "God help us." Aurel stepped down from the crate. "Then we go."

Pavel tugged at Teodora's sleeve. "Mama, is there a war up here?" She watched the salvage crew break apart toward the docking spurs, already arguing about fuel and spare seals, and then she turned her sons by the shoulders toward the lift marked for Ring Seven. "Not on this deck," she said. "Walk." Mihai went where she pushed him, but he looked back once at the empty crate, still holding the recruitment chit in his fist.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2dd771f1-7217-4c65-8867-db22c20ba100">Teodora took her sons up the lift to the hiring hall on Ring Seven.</li>
<li edge="3e50e882-1c17-4f38-8a7c-ba584607f12d">Aurel and his crew went down the docking spurs to their ship, the Halcyon.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2ee4966f-63e5-4284-9de7-bfb37edb0187" -->

The hiring hall of the Halcyon Ring smelled of welding flux and other people's breath, and the queue curled twice around the room before it reached the clerk's window. Teodora stood with the family's papers pressed flat against her chest while the clerk ran their names. The screen turned amber. Passage up had been paid, the clerk explained without looking at her, but berth and recycled water had not. Every liter the four of them had drunk since docking had been metered, filtered, and billed, and the sum glowing beside their names was more than Teodora had seen in one place in her life. She read it twice, then turned the screen so her husband could see it.

Mateo did not argue. He took the stylus from its chain and scrolled through the yard postings until he found the one paying best: ore haulers, fat-bellied freighters for the new mining outposts out among the asteroids, hull work on the outer cradles. He signed for a full contract, then signed again beneath it for double shifts, his hand steady through both signatures. Teodora watched the debt column recalculate. At sixteen hours a day it shrank to something that might end. She did not ask him when he meant to sleep. Around them the queue murmured the same rumor over and over, passed from mouth to mouth like a dropped tool. A shipping company down on the surface wanted to buy the station's charter. Some said that meant better wages, some said it meant every contract would be torn up, and none of them knew anything at all.

On the other side of the bulkhead, the records vault was cold and nearly silent, lit only by the terminals and the green telltales of the drives. Vance sat alone at the farthest console with his collar loosened and his jaw set. Geneva still sat in his chest like a swallowed stone. Through the wall he could hear the hall's low roar of worried families, people who had sold everything for a berth in the sky and now counted water by the liter. Frightened people, he had decided, did not want debate. They wanted a commander. Let the rival blocs seem to reach for each other's throats, let the alarms sound and the rumors turn to panic, and these same colonists would come to him and beg him to take emergency command. For a scare like that he needed the ciphers, and the ciphers belonged to one officer.

He went through the station rosters first, then the squadron files, then the sealed personnel annexes that his old codes still opened, reading clearance histories line by line the way another man might read a will. Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran's record was clean, so clean that he nearly passed it by. Then he noticed that one vetting entry carried a timestamp three days later than the inspector's signature beside it, and that the inspector named there had been off-station on that date. He pulled the original audit logs, laid them beside her file, and watched the forgery appear, a small and careful patch over a hole she had wanted no one to find. Vance copied both records to a private chip, cleared the access trail behind him, and sat back in the blue glow while, beyond the bulkhead, the hiring hall went on with its shuffling and its rumors.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="0823cf91-bb60-4ab8-b5be-17cbcb1ef64f">Mateo climbed to the public gallery of the station council chamber, where the councillors sat with visitors from the surface.</li>
<li edge="7493075f-2d3a-4d12-8d6b-727ec9d773d0">Vance pocketed the chip and took his place on the bridge of the Halcyon, bound for the debris field around Threnody.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="8d1b5b7e-e59d-479d-93ca-32fb9421fcd9" -->

The council chamber of the Halcyon Ring sat on the slow side of the station, where the spin gave a little less than a full Earth weight and the water in the delegates' glasses climbed the rims if anyone set them down too hard. Through the long curved port behind the dais the planet turned, blue and indifferent, the Andes a crumpled brown seam along its western edge. On the table lay a printed copy of the Geneva freeze, its signature page flagged in red: an armistice, now, and no longer a pause. Neither army could set foot in the lithium field. The councillors had passed it hand to hand that morning as though it were a relic.

The two agents from the Meridian Shipping Company did not sit. They stood at the foot of the table in grey travel coats still creased from the shuttle, and the elder of them spoke without notes. The Pacific bloc had launched a prospecting probe from its leased pad at Kourou, she said, bound for the asteroid catalogued as 2041 KX, a body rich in nickel and dense with lithium-bearing ore. The bloc had already filed a public survey claim. It named the Halcyon Ring as the staging point for any working of the rock. Meridian, she said, would consider carrying that ore, and it would consider which station it carried it through. She let her eyes travel along the scuffed walls, the patched ventilation grilles, the council members in their mended jackets, and said nothing more.

The vote took less than a quarter of an hour. The delivery date for the ore hauler moved forward by six weeks. The gantry safety crews, the spotters who watched the tethers and called the welders in when the solar weather turned, were cut to a single team for the entire outer ring. Councillor Abiodun read the consequences aloud in a flat voice for the record: the families whose berths and water rations were held against standing debt would supply the extra labor, in double shifts, on gantries no one would be watching. In the narrow public gallery above the chamber Mateo Quispe stood among the other indebted workers with his helmet ring still pressed red into his neck. He did not move when his family's berth number was read out with the rest. A woman beside him began counting hours on her fingers and stopped when she ran out of fingers.

At the far end of the table, three observers from the Andean coalition had sat through the whole proceeding with their tablets dark. When the gavel fell, the eldest of them rose and asked to be heard. Their intelligence, he said, was plain on the matter. The Pacific bloc had lost the salt flats at Geneva, and it did not intend to lose the war for battery metals along with them. It meant to win in orbit what it could no longer take on the ground, and the Halcyon Ring, whatever its council believed, had just become the ground. No one answered him. The Meridian agents were already gathering their coats, and beyond the port the brown seam of the mountains rolled slowly out of sight.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c7953f37-f0a0-4457-923e-ea3a926ef712">Mateo Quispe left the gallery and went back out to the shipyard gantries for his next shift.</li>
<li edge="9fac2ab9-dfa0-4f07-a54f-970508cd972d">Aboard his flagship off the station, Commodore Vance called Lieutenant Oyelaran to his private quarters.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1fdc4dba-7766-4f54-991b-2a330f87a58c" -->

The notice came down the station feeds at the start of second shift: the Meridian Shipping Company now held the Halcyon Ring's charter, and from the next cycle every family's air and water would be drawn against yard wages. Out on the gantries the men and women read it on their wrist slates between welds and said little. Mateo, Teodora's elder son, read it twice, then clipped his tether to the spine of the third ore hauler and kept working. He was eleven hours into a double shift. The haulers were promised to the new mining outposts beyond the Belt, and the bonus for finishing them early was the only number on the notice that meant anything to him.

The strike came without sound. A spray of Kessler debris, bolts and paint flakes and a shard of some dead satellite's mirror, crossed the yard at a speed no eye could follow, and the gantries bloomed with venting gas. A crane coupling burst. A hull plate the size of a cargo door tore loose from its cradle and swung on its last cable like a slow pendulum, and when it stopped it had Mateo against a girder, his suit hissing at the shoulder. On the common channel Vance was shouting: clear the lanes, get a line on him, somebody do something, over and over, louder each time.

The crew did something. Dov went hand over hand to the burst coupling with a cutting torch and rebuilt it from salvage on his belt, and when a seal flared back across his forearm he kept going until the clamp held. Yuna reached him there and braced him against the frame, talking low and steady into his helmet, holding him upright while his breath came ragged. Isa climbed into the half-built hauler, which had no hull plating aft and no flight systems but its raw thrusters, and fed it fuel by hand, nudging that unfinished bulk across the yard until its nose pressed the plate away from the girder and held it there. Lio hung from a gantry arm with a handheld scope and read the drift of the wreckage aloud, a gap opening here, closing there, until the rescue skiff could slip through to Mateo. They brought him in with his suit patched and his ribs cracked, alive.

In the airlock afterward Vance spoke to the yard feed while the crew were still peeling off their gloves. He described the plan he had made, the orders he had given, the lives he had held together by his own will. Dov sat on a bench with his arm in Yuna's lap and did not look up. Isa looked at Lio, and Lio looked away.

Through the lock port, past the drifting cloud of debris, the running lights of Threnody station hung small and cold, and docked at its outer ring was a ship whose markings everyone in the room knew. Vance saw it and his voice changed. Those were enemies, he said, and if they got in the way when the crew made their move, they would be left behind, the same as anything else that slowed them down. He had promised peace, once, at the start of all this, and he said it now as if he had never said the other. No one answered him. The crew sat in the smell of scorched seals and spent fuel, and for the first time they were thinking about what kind of man would be giving the orders when it came to that.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8a5e038b-a6e5-4d98-8230-e9387c3bb965">Teodora brought Mateo up the spoke elevator toward the infirmary ring.</li>
<li edge="7cf10033-646f-4868-ad75-92ad76f51acc">Isa took the Halcyon's controls for the approach to Threnody.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="bce06520-045c-43e8-b48c-d7ff84e47884" -->

The spoke elevator shuddered to a stop at the infirmary ring, and Teodora dragged Mateo out of it with one arm hooked under his shoulders. His boots scraped the deck plating. The spin gravity was heavier out here at the rim, and she felt every kilo of him in her lower back. Blood had dried brown along the cuff of his yard coveralls, and his breath came short and wet. The infirmary hatch stood ten paces off, its window glowing with clean white light, but a Meridian Shipping Company clerk stood in front of it with a ledger slate held flat against his chest, the way a deacon holds a hymnal.

"Account first," the clerk said. He turned the slate toward her. The new station ledger filled the screen in tidy columns. Beneath Mateo's name ran a line for the debtor, and beneath that a clause pledging the family's yard wages, all of them, for years not yet worked, against the cost of his care. Teodora read it once, and her mouth went thin. Her grandmother had signed something like it in a farm office on Earth, with the harvest standing for collateral, and the harvest had failed, and the land had gone to the company in the end. The terms on the screen were the same. Only the crop had changed. Mateo sagged against her hip. She took the stylus.

A man in a gray officer's coat leaned against the bulkhead beside the hatch and paid no attention to any of it. Vance had his wrist slate tilted up toward his face, and its screen was filling with traffic from both blocs, Concord fleet chatter and Ascendancy routing orders, scrolling past in plain text. The stolen cipher keys had stripped the encryption away. He stopped scrolling at a single line: a colonial supply convoy, twelve hulls, scheduled to cross the disputed lane in thirty hours. He tapped the lane on the slate, and it turned red. A war scare staged there would frighten the station badly enough, and when he answered it the Council would beg him to take command. He would come back as Halcyon Ring's rescuer. He smiled a little and marked the hour.

Mara stood two steps behind him, where she had been ordered to wait, with the convoy's navigation file open on her own slate. Plot the course, he had told her, as if it were nothing. She looked up from the screen and saw Teodora's hand moving across the ledger. Teodora's grip on the stylus was hard and steady, and she was signing away years she had not yet lived. Mara's eyes went back to the red lane on Vance's wrist and then to the convoy track she was drawing, and the two lines crossed. Those twelve hulls were full of families like this one, people who had pledged everything they had to get out here. Under her thumb, the course line ran straight into the place where the shooting would start.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="55b6248e-577a-43ce-9c41-7c22dc067e77">Teodora stayed beside Mateo's cot in the infirmary ring.</li>
<li edge="8c34160b-049e-4e02-8775-61ec5b3b4e53">Mara followed Vance aboard his flagship to Threnody station.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="16d9db42-4f9b-4de9-8dd9-48dadc284776" -->

The infirmary ring turned slowly enough that the water in the drip bags hung almost straight, and Teodora sat on a folding stool beside the cot with her hands pressed between her knees. Mateo's leg lay in a splint of gray foam from the hip to the ankle, the shin swollen dark under the dressings where the bulkhead had come down on it. He slept with his mouth open. A pump beside him ticked out its doses, and the air smelled of antiseptic and of the scrubbers working harder than they should.

The screens at the end of the ward, which usually showed the tide of cargo traffic around the station, had been taken over by a bulletin stamped with the seal of the Pacific bloc's intelligence directorate in Santiago. A man in a pressed collar read from a lectern while satellite photographs filled the frame behind him: an equatorial launch complex seen from straight above, fuel trucks drawn up in a neat crescent, a capture tug standing upright on its pad with frost blooming white along its tanks. The Andean coalition, the man said, was preparing to take 2041 KX, and its iron and nickel and platinum with it. The bloc would not stand by while that rock went to its rival and the lithium field stayed frozen under treaty. Its leaders had ordered their own tug fueled and held on the pad, to launch in the same minute the coalition's did. He said it the way a man reads a tide table.

While he spoke, three Meridian Shipping crewmen came along the corridor outside with a cart of steel boxes and a pneumatic driver. They stopped at every family's hatch in turn. Through the infirmary's open door Teodora watched them cut into the air line at the nearest berth, splice in a meter with a small amber window, and bolt it to the wall with four sharp cracks of the driver. Then they did the water line. One of them pasted a sheet beside the hatch and moved on to the next. When they reached the infirmary, he pasted the same sheet inside the doorframe. It said that, to meet the company's obligations under the Pacific bloc launch contract, consumption would now be measured per household, and that shifts lost to injury, together with all medical care rendered, would be charged against household rations until repaid.

Teodora read it twice. Then she looked at the meter in the corridor, its amber digits already turning, and she thought of the last well. It had sat at the bottom of a pump house on land that had blown away a little more every spring, and her father had kept it running with wire and prayer. She had signed it over in an office in a town where the dust lay drifted against the doors like snow, to a man who never lifted his eyes from the paper. The money had bought four berths on a lift to orbit and nothing more. On the screen the tug steamed on its pad, and the man in the collar thanked the people of the bloc for their resolve. Mateo stirred, said something she couldn't make out, and slept again. The pump beside him went on ticking, and Teodora sat and listened to it count.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e87e9982-b392-468c-a628-d46c1b01e50d">Teodora left Mateo sleeping and climbed to the Meridian ration office on the docking spine.</li>
<li edge="b434180e-d4b0-4bac-8578-098aa790cd37">Elsewhere in orbit, Mara Ost sat locked in the brig of Vance's flagship off Halcyon Station.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="cdf3b6aa-3f4c-43f0-b6ff-af2928dedc19" -->

The Meridian ration office hung on the docking spine like a tick on a vein, one narrow room with a counter and a clerk and a viewport the width of a coffin lid. Teodora had waited in the queue for most of a shift, behind ice miners still smelling of hydraulic fluid and cold, and when she reached the counter she set Mateo's chit down hard enough to make the clerk blink. A full week docked, she said. Seven days of rations gone for a seal that had failed on its own, through no hand of his. The clerk, a thin man with company piping on his collar, did not argue. He turned the ledger screen toward her and scrolled, slowly, as though he wanted her to see every line.

It was all there. Every family on Threnody was charged for its berth by the cubic meter, for its air by the breath-hour, for each liter of ice the company's tugs hauled in from the mining outposts, metered, priced, and carried forward month on month. Mateo's docked week was only a small red figure in a long red column, and beneath it ran the column for his berth and the column for his air, and below those the debt his father had left, still accruing. "It isn't a mistake," the clerk said quietly. "It's arithmetic." Teodora read the totals twice and said nothing.

Beyond his shoulder the viewport framed the tugs coming in, blunt-nosed and frosted, nudging their cargo nets against the unloading clamps while the brown curve of Earth turned beneath them, hazed and enormous and indifferent. Closer in, along the docking ring, small figures in company suits moved with cutting torches. Vance's men worked in pairs, as he had ordered, stripping the station of anything that could be sold or hauled away, and the blue points of their torches bit through strut after strut. Their line ran inward along the ring toward the plating that sheathed the reactor bay, and there they slowed and cut more carefully, leaving the hull scored and thin. From the counter it looked like nothing, a few sparks drifting off into the dark.

Behind her a miner swore at his own docked ration, then another, and soon the queue was muttering in a low, steady growl the clerk pretended not to hear. Teodora pocketed Mateo's chit and turned toward them. She said little in the open, only a word here and a glance there, and one by one the miners drifted out of the line and followed her down the service ladder, past the maintenance hatches, into the cramped crawlspace below the spine where Isa and Dov were waiting with Yuna and Lio and, against all habit, the engineers from the rival shift. They crouched among the conduits and spoke softly. The reactor core belonged to whoever could hold it, and together they could. Isa would handle the locks, Dov the coolant lines, Yuna the control relays, Lio the watch on the corridors, and the miners would do the heavy work they did better than anyone aboard. They traced the route on a dusty panel with their fingers, straight to the bay and along its outer wall, the very hull plates where, a few meters above their heads, the torches were still cutting.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="9c666dbd-a542-4350-9833-498a6698a33b">Teodora carried Mateo's chit and the miners' grievance down to the water reclamation deck.</li>
<li edge="d5870bf9-2e5e-431e-8f14-e9f090614723">Isa, Dov, Yuna and Lio climbed up to the docking ring, where the torches were still cutting.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="8fe18c14-f519-41ea-a2d4-d59f843e02da" -->

The reclamation deck smelled of chlorine and old pipe, and the tanks rose in ranks under the sodium lamps, each one wide enough to drown a house. Teodora stood on the catwalk above the third tank with her son beside her, his arm still strapped across his chest where the gantry chain had caught him. Below, the miners and yard crews filled every gap between the tanks, still in their grey coveralls with the Meridian stencil on the back. On the wall behind her the company meter glowed green, counting liters against wages in a column that never stopped moving. She told them what it had cost her boy: seven days' water for the crime of being hurt on a Meridian crane. She told them she had watched the wells go dry back home and signed her family's debt over to the company for a berth in the sky, and that she had not crossed four hundred thousand kilometers of vacuum to find the same ledger waiting. They would not read the meters, she said. They would not wear the tags. Let Meridian come down to the tanks and count the water by hand.

The crowd answered her with a sound that rolled off the tank walls and came back doubled. A welder near the front pried the cover off the nearest meter and held up its wires, and men cheered him. Someone began to bang a wrench against a pipe, and others took up the beat until the whole deck rang with it.

The public screen above the sluice gates had been showing the convoy's approach all evening, twelve blunt tankers in a line, each one marked with the station's crest. While the banging went on, two clouds of drive flares came into the frame from opposite edges, the blue of the Atlantic bloc and the white of the Pacific. Each was hailing the other on open channels, and each accused the other of moving on the colonies first. Then the drive flares became weapons fire, and the convoy lay between them. The tankers did not explode the way ships did in films. They split, and their water poured out into the dark as glittering fans of ice. The wrenches stopped, one at a time. Somebody near the sluice began to count the tankers aloud and gave up at nine.

Teodora still had the speaking horn in her hand, but no one was looking up at her now. A new voice came over the deck speakers, calm and well-schooled: Commodore Vance, his squadron holding station just beyond the ring, offering escort and protection and the discipline of a proper command. The same welder who had torn open the meter was the first to shout for him. Then the cry spread across the deck. Let the Commodore come. Let his marines hold the tanks. Let anyone hold them, so long as there was water. Teodora's son turned to her with his good hand open, as if she might have something to put in it. Above them the meter on the wall, its cover still hanging loose, went on counting.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="f53623d6-485c-4994-bced-3c7818c37919">The yard crews left the reclamation deck and made for the shipyard spine, where the half-built colony ships hung waiting in their cradles.</li>
<li edge="761d5de0-3f8b-44d6-b584-9cc2bfd05769">The engineers pulled from the convoy's wreckage were carried up to the medical bay, where Yuna waited with her sleeves pushed back.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5ab7549c-6af8-4c7c-9f6f-8da33d2c8248" -->

The shipyard spine of Halcyon Ring ran a kilometer and a half out from the hub, a gantry of white trusses where the colony ships hung in their cradles like gutted fish, ribs open to vacuum. At shift change the welders should have cycled through the spine locks in their usual staggered pairs. Instead they came all at once, two hundred and more, helmets under their arms, still carrying the damp mineral smell of the reclamation tank where the vote had been counted by raised hands. They left their torches clamped to the hull plates, left the half-seamed bulkheads glowing faintly as they cooled, and walked the length of the spine in silence toward the residential rings. Behind them the hulls of the *Amaru* and the *Concord* and the *Far Kestrel* went dark section by section.

The news screens along the spine corridor were all showing the same thing. The Andean capture tug had finished its spectral pass over 2041 KX, and the survey hung there in false color, a lumpy gray body with a thin vein of lithium-bearing ore threaded through it like a single red hair. The analysts' numbers scrolled beneath: enough to close one bloc's shortfall, not both. Before the crowd had reached the midpoint lock, the image changed to a tracking plot. A Pacific bloc craft had lit its engines and was burning hard on an intercept that would put it at the rock first. Within minutes Lima and Manila had issued statements in nearly identical language, each calling any interference with its vessel an act of war, and a ticker underneath announced that the Secretary-General had called both delegations to Geneva. A few of the welders stopped to watch. Most kept walking. Every one of them knew whose hulls were supposed to carry colonists out to wherever that lithium went.

Meridian's answer appeared on the ring-section displays an hour later and gave no explanation. Atmosphere supply to Sections C-4 through C-9, where the yard crews bunked with their families, dropped to a maintenance ration. The fans went quiet first, then the hiss in the vents thinned to a whisper, and the air took on a close, used taste. At the hub docks, a shuttle from Earth was already decelerating into its berth, and the manifest posted on the public board listed sixty-two contract welders, certified and bonded, with sixty-two more booked on the next ascent.

Teodora stood in the C-6 junction with her back against the bulkhead and watched the partial-pressure meter over the hatch. The number was falling steadily, one decimal place every few minutes, about as fast as a slow clock. She did the arithmetic the way she would have figured a weld bead, length against heat against time, and it came out the same each way. The yard crews could hold their sections closed and share what air they had, and Meridian could wait them out without lifting a finger. All of it, every breath on Halcyon Ring, came from the ice the miners hauled in from the outer belt and cracked into oxygen in the processing bays down-spine. Teodora watched the meter lose another tenth and understood it plainly. Unless the ice crews stopped with them, the strike would run out of air before Meridian ran out of welders.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="32ef61e0-2e24-4840-b751-d82470d7fd22">Teodora left the C-6 junction and rode a returning ice hauler out to the miners' dock at Threnody.</li>
<li edge="ddb87814-afc2-4a99-a53c-6da499d73ad2">The tracking plot on the news screens led to Halcyon's command deck, where Vance watched the two craft close on 2041 KX.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7666295e-bdbb-4ce8-8780-a4afdfe7e605" -->

The ice hauler *Brine Saint* came into Threnody's dock nose-first and groaning, its cargo frame packed with a blue-white slab of comet core the size of a chapel, and Teodora rode it in braced against the cab's rear bulkhead with a pressure suit unsealed to the collar. Through the scarred viewport the Kessler Belt turned slowly beyond the docking arms, a glittering haze of old satellites and broken hulls, and Threnody hung against it like a rusted lantern. When the clamps took hold and the hauler shuddered still, the unloading crews were already drifting toward the cargo frame with their cutters and tethers. Teodora pushed off the cab ladder and met them before the first cutter sparked.

She did not shout. She held up a ration chit from the shipyard galleys, the same pale green as the ones clipped to every miner's sleeve, each stamped with Meridian's meter code. Every liter of water they drank, she told them, every breath they pulled from the scrubbers, was counted against the ice they cut, and the same company counted the welders on Halcyon Ring the same way. The yards had already stopped. Without ice the Ring's air and water plants would run dry within weeks, and Meridian would have to come to the table or watch its whole orbit choke. An old cutter named Bartosz turned his chit over in his gloved fingers for a long moment, then switched off his torch and hung it on his belt. One by one the others did the same. By the next shift change four haulers sat in their cradles with their cargo frames still full, frost creeping over the ice where it met the station's warmer air, and the transfer lines to Halcyon Ring hung slack and silent.

In the cold lee of the idle haulers, Isa's crew spread a salvaged display sheet across a cargo crate, and the rival engineers gathered on the other side of it, thermal wraps still around their shoulders, their faces gray with exhaustion. Dov laid his seal schematics on the sheet, hand-annotated and smudged with grease, and the rivals overlaid their scans of the reactor housing: radiation contours, fracture lines in the shielding, the exact angle of the core's mounting struts. Where the two sets of plans met, a path appeared, one that would cut the core free without cracking its containment. Isa traced it with one finger. Two teams, she said, one on each side of the housing, and the core's output split down the middle afterward, half to the strikers' camp and half to the rival yard. Nobody argued. For the first time in that crowded dock, the two factions were bent over the same light.

Vance did argue, quietly, once the others had stepped back. He asked Dov for the seal plans, all of them, for his hands alone, since his people had the better torches and the steadier crews. Dov folded the schematics against his chest and said no. Vance smiled, nodded, and drifted away toward the supply racks. Only when Isa looked up from the display sheet a few minutes later did anyone notice that a cutting rig and a sealed radiation suit were missing from the racks, and that the service hatch leading inward to the reactor ring stood open and swinging. Bartosz was the first to grab a tether line. Then the miners came after him, and the strikers behind them, and the rival engineers pulled their wraps tight and followed, a long chain of bodies hauling themselves hand over hand down the corridor toward the reactor, their lamps throwing wild shadows ahead into the dark.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="b2c1cbaa-9dd1-46e7-89dd-d66bb7201f90">Far along the slack transfer lines, the colonists of Halcyon Ring crowded into the central concourse beneath the Meridian executive spindle.</li>
<li edge="1fb16b67-57a7-41ef-945d-e85aa73033f8">Isa, Dov and the rival engineers hauled themselves deeper through Threnody's maintenance shafts after Vance, toward the reactor.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="11e410ae-14ef-4e58-88ae-6b44eeffd9a6" -->

The central concourse of the Halcyon Ring had been built to impress visitors, and it still did: a long curving hall of brushed alloy and false sky, the Meridian executive spindle rising out of its center like the axle of a wheel. But the false sky had been dimmed to save power, and the air that moved through the vents tasted of metal and old breath. Beyond the viewports the ice haulers hung at their moorings, their holds sealed and full, untouched since the dockworkers had walked off. The crowd that filled the concourse had come from every ring of the station. They were miners and recyclers and hydroponics crews, and some had brought their children. They pressed against the barrier at the base of the spindle and called up to the gallery where Aurel Vance, chairman of the Meridian board, stood looking down at them.

A woman near the front, her lips split from the dry air, held up a ration card stamped with the week's water allowance and shouted that it would not keep a child alive. Others took up the cry. They wanted the haulers opened under their own crews' control, wages paid in water rather than scrip, and the Council's warships recalled from the approach lanes where they had sat for nine days with their guns trained on the docks. Vance let them shout until their voices began to crack. Then he spoke without raising his own. The Council's ships, he told them, had not come to protect them. Earth had let them go the day the last supply convoy turned back, and the warships were there to make sure the station's water never went down the well to people who could pay more for it. Meridian was all that stood between them and the vacuum, and he was Meridian. When he finished, the concourse was quiet except for the hiss of the vents.

He did not wait for an answer. In the gallery behind him, a logistics officer stood at a console with her hands folded, and Vance gave his orders as if he were reading out the date. The last of the stored ice was to be pumped from the central reservoir: one portion to the executive decks of the spindle, the rest to the long dark hull of the *Covenant*, the colony ship half-finished in the construction cradle off the station's spine. Its berths had been promised to families still waiting on the parched Earth, and the board meant for it to sail with its tanks full whether or not anyone on Halcyon lived to see it go. The officer entered the transfer, and somewhere beneath the concourse floor the pipes began to knock as the water moved away from the people standing over it.

Then Vance leaned close and gave one more order, quietly enough that the crowd below could not have heard it. The water meters on Ring Four, where most of the strikers lived, were to be cut. They would not be shut off entirely, only throttled, and unevenly, so that some corridors ran dry while others still trickled, with no notice and no explanation given. The officer hesitated only a moment before she complied. By the evening shift the first fights had started at the Ring Four standpipes, neighbor against neighbor, each accusing the other of hoarding, of bargaining privately with the board, of informing. The leaders who had stood at the barrier that morning were shouted down in their own corridors. Within two days the dockworkers began drifting back to the haulers one by one, each hoping to be counted among the loyal before the next meter went dark, and the strike broke apart under the weight of their fear of one another.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="229321ea-b3fc-4b96-9180-529c78356918">Teodora and the striking yard crews stayed in the habitat ring, under the long windows that looked out past the station.</li>
<li edge="91430894-a496-4977-874a-bfc2d1e312a8">Vance's officers followed him down to the life-support depot, where the reactor corridor ran to the core housing.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5b6d4e2b-ba4a-4ec2-9e94-2b817c21e71a" -->

Through the long windows of the habitat ring, 2041 KX hung where no sky should have held anything so large. It was a dark, pitted lump the size of a city district, and two craft were grappled to it like ticks on a dog. On the near flank the Andean tug burned its thrusters in short orange coughs. On the far flank the Pacific bloc craft answered with blue ones. Neither would release its clamps, because neither government would admit that the last nickel-iron worth a war could belong to anyone else. Between them they had dragged the rock into high orbit beside Halcyon, and every hour the station's speakers relayed the same two orders in two languages: the shipyards would build outposts on the asteroid, and they would build them now.

The shipyards built nothing. The gantries along the outer hull stood frozen in the positions they had held when the strike began weeks earlier, their welding arms folded like sleeping birds. Out past the docking collar, the ice haulers from the mining outposts drifted in a patient queue, still laden, their tanks frosted white, because no one would unload them. In the ring the miners and yard crews slept in shifts in the corridors, played cards on overturned crates, and painted slogans on the bulkheads in sealant gray.

Then the air began to thin. It started as a pressure in the ears and a faint hiss from the vents that had always breathed out and were now drawing in. Children yawned and could not stop. An old hauler named Quispe sat down hard against a wall and said, to no one in particular, that the surface companies had done the same thing with water in the drought towns, turning a valve somewhere far away and waiting to see who would come begging first. The readouts by the hatches showed the number falling in neat, bloodless increments. Meridian had not sent a message. It did not need to.

Teodora climbed onto a cargo sled so that the people at the back of the corridor could see her, and she did not raise her voice, because shouting wasted breath. She pointed up the nearest spoke toward the hub, where the shipyard control spindle turned in its housing with the station's air and water valves inside it. Then she started climbing. The miners followed in their scuffed pressure vests, and the yard crews followed with cutting torches and pry bars still stenciled with Meridian's logo. They went hand over hand up the ladders, and as they rose toward the hub the spin eased off their bodies until they were swimming more than climbing. Above them, framed in every porthole of the spoke, the contested asteroid turned slowly. The two little ships were still fastened to it and still pulling against each other, as if the rock were the only thing in the sky worth holding on to.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a79bbf26-8fdf-4d70-b929-dce99151832e">Up the spoke, the climbers followed Teodora toward the spindle's sealed hatch.</li>
<li edge="41af2a9b-7369-40a2-9901-9dec8fc2ddfc">On the command deck, Vance's officers gathered to watch the tugs and the feeds from Earth.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="6c92fd8b-c722-4f89-96df-1b0837f58484" -->

The alarms had climbed half an octave by the time the climbers reached the upper third of the central spoke, and with every rung the Halcyon Ring let go of them a little more. Down at the rim the spin had held them firmly to the deck. Up here near the hub, bodies drifted from the ladder between pulls, and boots found nothing to stand on. Teodora went first, hand over hand, her miners strung out below her in their scuffed pressure suits, the yard crews behind them with torch rigs lashed to their backs. Climbing with them, never quite within arm's reach, came the engineers in the grey coveralls of the other faction, and among them a lean woman with a flight patch torn from her shoulder who had once flown for Vance. Nobody spoke about who belonged to which side. The red strobes spun against the curved walls and lit every face the same.

The spindle hatch was a slab of layered alloy, sealed and silent. They hung around it like moths. Teodora pressed her glove to the frame and named the seals one by one, which cycled on pressure, which on command, and which would fight back if anyone cut in the wrong order. The grey-coveralled engineers looked at one another. Then the eldest of them unrolled a schematic no one outside their faction had ever been shown: coolant lines threaded behind the spindle's plating, a whole hidden circulatory system. The pilot traced the hull with one finger and marked where the plating had been patched, where micrometeor scarring had thinned it, and where a torch would bite fastest. With the three accounts laid over each other, the hatch was a problem with an answer. The yard welders anchored themselves to the ladder rails, struck their shipbuilding torches, and began to cut along the pilot's line, and white sparks floated off in slow, unhurried arcs.

Teodora pulled back to the spoke wall and keyed the long-range band. "Haulers, this is Teodora. Break off from the outposts. Turn around, bring the ice in, and hold the docks. Nobody leaves them until you hear from me." The answers came in one at a time, scratchy with distance, and every one of them was yes. Then the spoke's intercom crackled over the hiss of the torches, and Vance's voice filled the shaft, smooth and close. He spoke only to her. There was a pod, he said, fueled and clear, and a berth on it for one. All she had to do was give him the seal sequence and let the rest of them sort out the station. The welders' torches dipped. The engineers watched her, and so did the pilot. Teodora reached up, found the intercom panel, and switched it off without a word. Then she turned toward the opening hatch.

The last seam parted and the slab swung inward on a breath of escaping air. Teodora led them through and along the engineers' schematic to the coolant junction, a knot of valves and frost-rimed piping at the spindle's heart. Meridian's guards were there, six of them, braced against the stanchions with their weapons up, and the climbers kept coming out of the hatch behind her, miners and welders and grey coveralls, until the guards were outnumbered four to one. The guards' sergeant held his ground long enough to say that the security cutter was coming. The pilot answered him flatly that the cutter was still days out from Earth, and he knew it. The sergeant looked at the faces filling the junction, then at the valves behind him. He lowered his weapon first, and the others followed. The spindle and every valve in it changed hands, and above them the alarms went on climbing.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="52bdf3e4-fdbe-468a-b5fc-b9c2e999933c">At the crown of the control spindle, Vance stood over the boards of the communications array.</li>
<li edge="c0c0d4a2-c3bd-4750-8bce-0f478c81ecba">Far out in the Kessler Belt, the envoys met at the reactor coolant junction of Threnody station.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5820fe5e-d2ec-44bd-a236-2d1ec5dd6e11" -->

The communications array occupied the narrow crown of the shipyard control spindle, a ring of consoles under a dome scratched dull by years of micrometeorite grit, and from the central board Vance held all of Halcyon Ring in his hands. He keyed the scrubber banks back to life one sector at a time, and the pressure graphs for the habitat ring climbed out of the red like slow green tides. Then he opened the water valves, every deck, every manifold, no ration locks, and the feeds from the lower levels filled with the sound of it: pipes knocking as they flooded, people shouting over the hiss of taps that had been dry, someone laughing until the laughter broke into something else. The officers at the side consoles watched him and did not speak.

On the long-range display a single hard return was crawling out from the blue curve of Earth: Meridian's security cutter, running lights burning, transponder broadcasting company authority on every band. Vance did not have to give the order. The ice haulers, slab-sided and pitted, their tanks still crusted white from the outer belt, swung their bulk across the docking mouths one after another until the cutter's approach vector closed on nothing but scarred hull. Within the hour the station's director came up the spindle ladder alone, his company jacket buttoned wrong, and set a data slate on the board beside Vance's hand. The station codes were on it. He

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="bcc458e8-9b14-4635-a366-919835d369f7">Down in the habitat ring commons, the crews gathered at the long window that looked over Earth.</li>
<li edge="aa282580-88f4-4a56-9ef3-835f8a938fa7">Up at the central board, Vance kept his hand on the station, all the way down to its reactor core.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="6a7825cb-ec86-4eac-a445-17431dec49db" -->

The commons of the Halcyon Ring curved upward at either end until the floor became a wall and the wall became a ceiling, and along its outer arc ran the long window that looked down on Earth. That night the planet turned beneath it with its terminator crawling west across the equatorial strait, and the crews who had packed the commons shoulder to shoulder, miners still in their pressure liners and yard hands with welding scorch on their sleeves, watched the coastlines go dark. It was not nightfall. City by city the lights along both shores of the strait winked out in ragged blocks, as if someone were pinching them off between finger and thumb. On the speaker panels above the galley hatch a relay loop repeated the same two notices: the Meridian consortium had suspended all food shipments to Halcyon until the yards returned to work, and the governments of both surface blocs, which agreed on nothing else, had each declared the strike a mutiny against lawful authority.

Then the launches began. From the dark coasts came points of white fire, one and then six and then more than anyone in the commons could count, climbing out of cities that had shut off their own pumps and grids to feed the pads. The blocs were racing each other, not the strikers. Station modules rose on heavy boosters, and between them flew the armed tugs, blunt and fast, their running lights flicking past the ring close enough that the window rattled in its frame when their wakes of exhaust crossed the hull. None of them slowed. They burned outward toward 2041 KX, toward the iron-nickel bulk of the rock whose dense end everyone on the station knew to the meter, each bloc meaning to plant its flag on the richer half before the other could. A yard foreman named Okafor said aloud what most of them were thinking: the people below were sitting in the dark without water so that someone could own metal they had never cut.

The vote was taken by hands, because the tally board had been rerouted to inventory. The motion to keep striking carried without a single hand raised against it, and the motion to ration followed: two meals a day from the cold stores, protein vats run at full cycle, and the hydroponics bays opened to anyone with a free shift. While the quartermasters were still chalking the ration figures on the bulkhead, the comms officer climbed onto a galley counter and turned up the receiver. The lunar ice outposts were calling, Shackleton and Peary and the three small drill camps on the far side, their voices thin with lag and static. They were joining the strike, they said. The water would keep coming on the regular schedule, mass driver to ring, and no one on Earth would be paid for it.

The charter had been lying on the long table at the center of the commons for most of the night, forty pages printed on salvaged polymer sheet and weighted at the corners with torque wrenches. Someone read the final clauses aloud over the noise of the departing tugs, and when the hands went up a second time the count was no closer than the first. Four of them carried it out of the commons together, a miner, a welder, a hydroponics tech, and a girl from the ice-loading crew who could not have been older than seventeen, and the crowd parted to let them through to the spoke lift. Weight fell away from them as the car climbed toward the hub. Above, in the spindle where the ring's gravity faded to nothing, Teodora was waiting for them with a pen, while beyond the window the last of the night launches dwindled into the dark toward the rock.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="d3c7b358-deb7-421f-98fd-4b2b07f3db81">The charter went with Teodora down to the reactor core beneath the shipyard control spindle.</li>
<li edge="41f436be-590b-40b2-94bf-311806a3740f">The night launches burned on toward 2041 KX, watched from the governor's command deck of Halcyon Station.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="cb753a19-feb2-4ec4-b459-bbac46411575" -->

The reactor core of Halcyon Ring sat beneath the shipyard control spindle like a stone in a throat, and on that shift it was failing. Coolant lines sweated frost along the catwalks, and the containment field threw a bruised violet light across the faces of the people working the manual valves. Some wore the grey coveralls of Vance's old crew; others wore the rust-orange of the engineers who had once cut their hoses and welded their hatches shut. Now they stood shoulder to shoulder at the bypass wheels, bodies thin from weeks without full rations, calling pressure readings to one another in two dialects of the same tired trade. When a seal on the third loop began to scream, a grey-sleeved woman and an orange-sleeved man threw their weight against the same wheel without a word, and the needle crept back out of red.

Above them, behind a pane of armored glass, sat the spindle's control room, and Vance was in it. He had locked the doors himself, keyed the bulkheads around the core so that whoever stood below could not reach the upper systems, and when the core began to destabilize the lock had held him as surely as it held them. He pressed his palm flat against the glass. His voice came down through a cracked speaker grille, first commanding, then reasoning, then only saying their names, one after another. No one looked up. The release panel for his door lay forty meters below, within reach of a dozen hands, and every one of those hands stayed on a valve.

The core's hum dropped by slow degrees, from a shriek to a growl to something close to breathing. When the last loop settled into green, Teodora climbed onto the main console with a data slate held against her chest. Her knuckles were split and her hair was stiff with dried coolant. She read the charter aloud, its plain clauses stating that the station's air, its water, and its power belonged to no shipping company, that Meridian's meters would be torn out of every conduit, and that each faction aboard would draw from the same lines by the same measure. Then she pressed her thumb to the console's reader, and the reactor itself logged her signature beside the core temperature and the flow rates, as if it were one more reading.

A ragged noise rose from the catwalks, too hoarse to be a cheer. Teodora didn't join it. She looked past the people embracing on the gantries to the long viewport at the base of the spindle, where Earth hung blue and enormous and the running lights of distant traffic moved across the dark. Meridian's lawyers were down there, and so were the governments that leased them their lanes, and she knew that a signature on a reactor console meant nothing to a fleet. She also knew that by now someone would be drafting the order to take the station back. She stepped down from the console and went to help coil a hose. Behind the glass overhead, Vance stood with his hand still pressed to the pane, and the room around him stayed quiet.

<!-- cell: markdown id="7be5e79a-f693-49a1-b248-879ed67734c3" -->

The salt flat ran white to the edge of the world, crusted into hexagons that cracked underfoot like old porcelain, and across it, in the hard noon of the Atacama, two armies had stopped a kilometer apart. To the west the Pacific bloc's columns sat in grey ranks behind their armored carriers; to the east the Andean coalition had dug ochre trenches into the crust and planted their banners in the brine. Between them lay the evaporation ponds of the last rich field, rectangle after rectangle of water so blue it looked poisonous, slowly giving up its lithium to the sun. A line of surveyor's stakes marked the concession border, and on that line the Unified Council had raised a white pavilion whose canvas snapped in the wind coming down off the volcanoes.

Commodore Aurel Vance stood beneath it in full dress blues, the orbital service's silver comet on his collar, his boots already filmed with salt. He had come down from the high stations three days earlier on the understanding, never written but often repeated, that the governorship of the Orbital Colonies would be settled here and that it would be settled on him. The Council's delegation arrived by white tiltrotor, five envoys in pale linen, and their spokeswoman, a soft-voiced woman named Ostrander, read the terms of the truce into a microphone wired to loudspeakers that carried her words to both lines at once. The field would be worked jointly. The output would be divided by tonnage, under Council inspection. And as surety of good faith between the blocs, the Governor of the Orbital Colonies would be Tomasa Quispe of the Andean coalition.

For a moment the only sound was the wind. Then the eastern trenches erupted, a roar that rolled across the flat and broke against the pavilion, rifles raised, ochre banners swinging. From the western ranks came nothing. Vance felt the silence of his own side settle on his shoulders heavier than the roar. Ostrander turned to him with the microphone still live and asked, kindly, whether the Commodore would offer the Governor-designate his salute, so that both armies might see the orbital service accept the peace. Quispe stood two meters away, small and composed in a borrowed field jacket, and did not quite meet his eyes. Vance raised his hand to his brow. The loudspeakers carried the click of his heels to ten thousand soldiers.

He held the salute longer than regulation required, and while he held it he looked past Quispe and past the envoys at the turquoise ponds and the stakes and the cracked white ground, and he understood that none of it was theirs to give. The Council traded the colonies as it traded tonnage, for a quiet afternoon on a salt flat, and the blocs took what was thrown to them and cheered. Not one of the people under that canvas had ever watched a hull breach seal itself in vacuum, or rationed air through a solar winter, or kept nine hundred lives turning on a ring with nothing beneath them but dark. He had. When he lowered his hand his face showed nothing at all, and the envoys thanked him, and Quispe nodded, and the wind went on scouring the salt. Vance walked back toward the grey lines alone, and the conviction he carried with him across that white kilometer was simple and entire: the frontier had been given away by people who had never seen it, and only he had ever truly deserved to rule it.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="937dfcef-6fb4-4741-9477-3951b11e8f11">Vance went out to the Kessler Belt, to the navigation bay of the salvage ship Halcyon.</li>
<li edge="38988c63-3430-4630-87b7-be5b35aa48d0">Vance went back up to high orbit, to the Halcyon Ring station and the records vault beside its shipyard hiring hall.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a4fd0580-f753-400f-bc9d-e109185f38ad" -->

The relay came through at the top of the shift, a flat compressed voice from the treaty hall in Geneva crackling out of the navigation bay's speaker while the Halcyon drifted among the tumbling wreckage of the Kessler Belt. The Pacific bloc envoys had rejected any plan to share the lithium field's output, the voice said, and so had the Andean coalition, both delegations worn thin by months of skirmishes along the concession border. The mediators had managed only a freeze: neither side would set foot on the field, and each would have to find its battery metals somewhere else. The message ended with a burst of static, and no one in the bay reached to replay it.

Lio had the star charts spread across the plotting table, their edges curling under the cold light, and was tracing a long arc around the densest knot of debris with one finger. Beside him Isa scrolled through the Halcyon's old flight logs, calling out the dates and headings of every safe passage the ship had made through this stretch of the Belt, while Dov sat hunched at the sensor console, coaxing the forward array into a narrower, finer sweep. Each time Isa read a heading, Lio marked it; each time Dov's screen cleared a lane of drifting hull plate and frozen fuel, Lio extended the arc a little further. By the time the coffee had gone cold in its bulbs, a route to Threnody lay pencilled across the charts, slow and wide and threading only the gaps the three of them had confirmed together.

Then Dov's console chimed. A second contact had resolved at the edge of the sweep, a drive signature none of them needed to look up: the rival faction's ship, burning hard on a heading that pointed straight at Threnody. Dov read the numbers aloud, and the bay went quiet enough to hear the air scrubbers ticking in the walls.

Vance had been leaning in the hatchway, arms folded, saying nothing. Now he crossed to the plotting table, looked once at the long pencilled arc, and keyed his command code into the helm. The route vanished from the main display. In its place he drew a single straight line, cutting directly through the heart of the debris field. "We take the short way," he said. "Whoever docks at Threnody first owns its power, and I intend that to be me." Lio opened his mouth, and Isa set down her slate, but the override light already glowed red above the helm, and the Halcyon's engines had begun to climb.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e4f4f678-2e9e-445b-908a-169eb1ffa552">The news from Geneva reached the council chamber of the Halcyon Ring, where the station's councillors gathered to read it.</li>
<li edge="3346eabc-5b94-4958-ad4e-dd7249c6c7ba">The Halcyon swung onto Vance's straight line, and her crew took their stations on the bridge as the debris field drew near.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="76a1867d-ef9f-40e5-a1ed-2c6ded870ccf" -->

The quarters aboard the *Corvine* were the largest on the flagship and still too small for Commodore Elias Vance's temper. He had pinned the Council's reply to the bulkhead above his desk, a single paper sheet in an age of screens, so that he would see its three polite sentences every time he looked up. Halcyon Station turned slowly beyond the viewport, a wheel of lit windows against the dark, and he kept his back to it. Lieutenant Mara Oyelaran stood at attention by the hatch because he had not told her to sit.

He slid a data slate across the desk. On it was her own transmission log, the private relay she had kept open to her sister on the Ceres agricultural ring, routed through a military channel she had no right to use. "Eleven months," Vance said. "A court-martial would call that a security breach. I'd call it a family matter, if you gave me reason to." She read it twice, her jaw working, and then she took a thin chip from the inner pocket of her uniform and set it beside the slate. Both blocs' diplomatic keys, Andean and Pacific, sealed under her clearance. He thanked her as though she had brought him coffee.

The feed resolved in grainy blues on the wall display: a long table in Quito, mountain light through tall windows, ministers and generals with the gray faces of people who had not slept. They spoke Spanish and the decryption laid English beneath them half a second late. The armistice held, one of them said bitterly, and that was the trouble. After all the months of skirmishing along the salt flats, neither army could touch the lithium field, and the coalition's battery reserves fell every week on a chart nobody at the table wanted to look at. A general struck the wood with his palm. They would not hand the field to the Pacific bloc by sitting still while their grid went dark. If the ground was closed to them, they would take the metal from the sky. The minister at the head of the table gave the order without raising her voice: the engineers were to ready the capture tug for 2041 KX.

Vance leaned back and watched the orbital plot the feed had pulled up, a thin arc of an asteroid tumbling sunward. Mara had not moved from the hatch. "Sir," she said, "if you push a war scare out of this, and the colonies believe it, people will run. Evacuations kill. Panic on a habitat ring kills faster than any tug." He did not look away from the display. He lifted one hand from the armrest and turned it over, a small dismissive motion, the way a man brushes away a draft. The room went quiet except for the half-second lag of the translators in Quito.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fd836e34-5236-4fdf-9388-c38a21bdd210">The Halcyon set out across the Kessler Belt after the Andean tug, bound for 2041 KX.</li>
<li edge="bd777283-8416-4651-98b6-a6392c953921">Mara followed Vance off the Corvine to the infirmary ring hatch of Halcyon Ring station.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2ad11d8e-5086-455d-98c5-1111198d236a" -->

The fuel readout on the Halcyon's console had stopped showing numbers and begun showing a single amber bar, thin as a fingernail, that flickered whenever the patched engine coughed. Ahead, Threnody hung against the debris haze of the Kessler Belt, a skeletal docking frame clamped to the dark shoulder of 2041 KX, its guide lights blinking in patient sequence. The Andean coalition had poured every litre of hydrogen it could find into its capture tug, hydrogen pulled from the rationed power plants of its own cities and pumped on an open pad while foreign satellites passed overhead and photographed every hose. The tug had gone on ahead. The Halcyon had what was left, and twice now Isa had started the docking burn, and twice the engine had sputtered and died before the closing rate came down. The rock was turning beneath them, and the frame was turning with it.

The hail had been sitting on the comms panel for eleven minutes, a green Pacific bloc transponder code tagged to a ship holding station four kilometres off Threnody's far arm. Vance had silenced it the moment it appeared and gone aft to curse at the engine as though it might listen. When the hatch sealed behind him, Lio looked at Dov, Dov looked at Yuna, and Yuna looked at Isa, who still had both hands on the controls and her eyes on the closing rate. No one said much. Lio said that the bloc ship was drawing on batteries that were nearly gone and still had not moved off. Dov said that if they missed the frame a third time there would be no fourth. Yuna reached over and opened the channel, and Isa nodded once without turning her head.

The voice that answered was calm and spoke accented English, and within a minute two tow lines came spooling out of the bloc ship's bay, their magnetic heads trailing faint blue tracers across the Halcyon's forward glass. The rival pilot called the numbers slowly: roll two degrees port, hold, pitch down a hair, hold, and Isa matched every call so closely that the lines struck the hull grapples almost in the same instant. The cockpit shuddered. Dov counted the distance aloud while the bloc ship backed toward Threnody and drew them in behind it, its battery indicator visibly sagging on the shared telemetry, until the docking collar met the frame with a deep iron clank that went through the deck and into everyone's teeth.

Vance came through the hatch while the seal lights were still turning green. He saw the open channel, the tow telemetry, and the bloc ship's registry glowing on the main screen, and his face went the colour of old brick. He shouted that they had crawled on their knees to the enemy, that every one of them now owed their breath to the people they had come out here to beat, that the bloc would hold this over the coalition until the end of the race and beyond it. No one argued. Isa kept her hands on the dead controls, Yuna closed the channel with a quiet word of thanks that Vance cut off halfway through, and outside the glass the two tow lines slackened and drifted loose, still fastened to the hull.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ef5f9694-485b-4120-82dc-efc702369685">Far back in high orbit, on the Halcyon Ring station, Teodora kept watch in the infirmary beside Mateo's cot.</li>
<li edge="4cbf451a-dc98-481b-80e6-a70df680e2e1">Once the seal lights held green, the crew left Vance in the cockpit and passed through the docking collar into Threnody's salvage corridor.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7d9d0e2e-c24c-4d3d-9aa8-ec27632e3d6c" -->

The brig of the flagship was three cells and a corridor, lit the flat white of a ship that never slept. Mara Ost sat on the bunk behind the transparent door of the middle cell, her wrists free but her comm stripped from her collar, and watched Admiral Vance stand in the corridor with his back to her. He had ordered the relay put up on the bulkhead screen where she could see it too, and he did not turn around while it played. Through forty seconds of lag and two hops of encryption, the Andean coalition's war cabinet sat around a long table in Quito, under a ceiling of carved wood, with the afternoon light of a world below them falling across their papers.

The defense minister spoke first. The Pacific bloc had fueled a tug, she said, and was holding it on the pad at Kiribati. It had been sitting there since morning, crewed and ready. A man at the far end of the table raised the lithium field, and the room let out a tired breath; the Atacama talks were deadlocked and would stay deadlocked through the season, and everyone there knew it. If they waited, the finance minister said, the bloc would have 2041 KX and its nickel and platinum in hand before the lithium question was even settled, and the coalition would be bargaining from nothing on both fronts. There was no vote, only a look passed around the table and a nod from the president at its head. The tug at Guayaquil would launch at the next equatorial window. It would claim the asteroid first.

Mara was on her feet before the feed cut. "Admiral, the convoy is already on that trajectory. Two tugs racing for the same rock, both of them armed, and the colony ships are right there in the middle of it. There are families aboard. Children." Vance turned then, slowly, and looked at her through the door as if she had told him something quaint. "The colonists will thank whoever saves them," he said. "They always do, afterward."

The comm panel chimed before she could answer, and the face of a Council envoy filled the screen where Quito had been: a narrow, careful man in the pale grey of the civil service, smiling the way people smiled when they had been sent to smooth something over. The Council, he said, recognized the Admiral's long service and his standing in the outer squadrons, and would be pleased to offer him a seat as deputy undersecretary for orbital logistics, effective at the next session. Vance listened to the whole of it without moving. Then he said, "No," and closed the channel while the envoy was still drawing breath. For a moment the corridor was quiet except for the hum of the ventilation. "Deputy," he said to no one, and almost laughed. He keyed the bridge. "Lieutenant Sato. Ready the dispatches for release, the full set, and route them through the Halcyon relay so they carry the station's stamp." Behind the transparent door, Mara put her palm flat against it and said his name, but he had already walked away down the white corridor, and the door to the brig sealed shut behind him.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8a99d1bb-1bdd-422f-bcc5-47e23b9e49a1">The coalition's tug lifted from Guayaquil at the next equatorial window and made for the docking ring of Threnody station.</li>
<li edge="90694cd5-51bd-4261-aa51-7a81731ba919">Vance's dispatches went out under the Halcyon relay's stamp and reached the station's water reclamation deck, where Teodora stood with her son.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="93c01342-9df6-44c0-bd22-fc9b79bc837a" -->

The capture tug *Condor de Hierro* came into Threnody's docking ring on cold thrusters, its grapple arms folded against its belly like the legs of a dead insect. Below, on Earth, the lithium field lay frozen between two armies that could not dig without being shot, and the Andean coalition had sent this tug to end the shortage from above: strip the abandoned station's reactor core, burn for the metal-rich asteroid 2041 KX, and haul home what no trench could take. Isa eased the tug against the collar, and the hull rang with a contact that should have been silent. Another ship already hung at the far berth, its flank stenciled with the blue wave of the Pacific bloc. Their engineers were aboard. They had launched within hours of the coalition, and the fight over Earth's last metals had become a race for orbit.

The breach opened on the Pacific side of the ring. A seam split along the old station's spine, and the atmosphere went roaring out through a gash no wider than a hand, dragging loose bolts and frost with it. Behind a buckled pressure door, four rival engineers were sealed in a shrinking pocket of air, their helmet lamps swinging wildly through the viewport. Vance watched the readout fall and said what a commander says: leave them, take the core, go. Nobody moved to obey. Lio was already at the hatch, Dov already had the sealant gun charged, and Yuna was cramming the trauma kit into a tether bag while Isa's hands settled on the thruster controls.

Isa held the *Condor* against the station's shudder, feathering the jets so the docking collar would not tear loose as Threnody began to roll. Lio went first through the dark corridors, calling turns over the open channel and reading the warped deck plates by touch, finding the one crawlspace that still connected to the trapped section. Dov wedged himself against the gash with the gale screaming past his visor and laid the patch in three overlapping strips until the roar thinned to a whistle and then to nothing. Yuna reached the engineers as the pressure door gave. One had a cracked faceplate and blood freezing at the seal, another had a wrist bent the wrong way. She sealed the first and splinted the second in the narrow crawlspace, and then all of them came back along Lio's route in a slow chain of linked tethers. Four strangers in blue-wave suits floated alive into the coalition's airlock.

Vance watched it from the command couch. His crew pulled helmets from their enemies' heads and passed them water, and Dov laughed at something one of the Pacific engineers said in a language he barely spoke. Nobody looked toward the couch. Vance turned to the schematic of Threnody's reactor bay, still glowing amber on his screen. He studied the release sequence, the cradle locks, and the single access corridor that ran to it, and he decided that the core would not be shared with the bloc and would not be shared with his crew. He would take it for himself alone.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="390fd0fb-89be-466f-9907-bbc3bd3a6c3e">Out on the shipyard spine of Halcyon Ring, Teodora waited with the yard crews for the shift to change.</li>
<li edge="f6dfb233-c263-4651-a03b-ef040c5107ba">In the station's medical bay, Yuna laid out her kit beside the injured Pacific engineers.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e6395f11-8cb0-450f-8038-6e41cf3091a9" -->

The command deck of Halcyon Station hummed with the low, tired note of machinery running past its service interval. Across the main display, two points of light crept toward a third: the Andean tug *Cóndor de Plata* from one side, the Pacific hauler *Tenryū Maru* from the other, and between them the slow, tumbling lump of 2041 KX, its iron-nickel hide catching the sun in dull flashes. Beneath the tracking plot, a split window carried the faces of three United Nations mediators in Geneva, their words arriving a beat behind their lips.

The senior mediator, a gray-haired woman with a careful voice, laid out the compact: shared grapple, shared refining, the yield divided by a formula both blocs had once signed in a calmer year. The Andean delegate heard her out and shook his head before the delay had finished carrying her last sentence. The ore in that rock, he said, would not close half of Lima's deficit, let alone half of anyone else's. The Pacific delegate said almost exactly the same thing a moment later, with Osaka in place of Lima. Within the minute, both claims were lodged with the registry, each naming its own bloc as sole owner, and both crews received the same order in two languages: close, grapple, and hold, whatever the other ship did. On the plot the two lights did not slow.

Commander Vance watched it from the central chair without a word. He had held that seat only a short while, and he sat in it as though it had been built around him. When the communications officer turned and reported a second signal, a relief flotilla dispatched by the Council to stand in for the convoy that had never arrived, its lead ship requesting docking clearance and a berth schedule, Vance let her finish. Then he told her to deny it. Halcyon was managing, he said. The Council had not listened when he warned them, and it would not arrive now, at the end, to stand beside him in the record. The people aboard this station would know who had kept them alive.

The communications officer relayed the refusal in a flat voice. On a side panel no one had asked to see, the life-support figures ticked on regardless: oxygen reserve at nineteen days and falling, potable water rationed to the second tier, the scrubbers in Ring C flagged amber for the third shift running. Somewhere below the deck, the recyclers labored audibly. The relief flotilla held position for a long moment, then began a slow turn away. On the main display, the *Cóndor* and the *Tenryū* drew closer to the asteroid, and to each other, and Vance kept his eyes on them as if the station's air were already his to give.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="b45aec4e-a4ac-4764-b203-1cf68860ff99">The *Cóndor de Plata* and the *Tenryū Maru* burned on toward 2041 KX and the station buried in its rock.</li>
<li edge="567aab04-3839-4cc2-912c-fae4b4b0bec7">Commander Vance kept his chair as Halcyon's air and water ran lower among the people below his deck.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c727c6c2-1465-4d80-98ec-1b1707ce0421" -->

The maintenance shafts of Threnody ran through the rock of 2041 KX like wormholes through old timber, too narrow for two suits abreast and lit only by the amber strips that still drew power from the station's failing grid. Frost had grown on the conduit housings in pale, feathered sheets. Somewhere above, the tug that had brought Isa's crew sat docked with its reserve tank drained to the last bar, and beside it the Pacific bloc's intercept craft sat no better off. Neither ship was going anywhere, and everyone in the shaft knew it. Eleven people hauled themselves hand over hand along the rungs: five in the rust-orange suits of the Andean coalition, six in Pacific grey, none of them talking more than they had to.

The first hatch stopped them at the junction where the shaft bent toward the core. Someone had run a bead of weld along every seam, still faintly warm on the thermal overlay, the metal puckered and bright where the torch had bitten. The second hatch, visible through the junction grating, had been sealed the same way. A Pacific engineer named Oyelaran unclipped a scanner from her chest rig and swept it across the bulkhead, and the readout bloomed with a lattice of voids and ducts. "Coolant bypass," she said, tapping a channel that curled beneath the welded section and rejoined the main shaft forty meters on. "Two cuts. One here, one at the far end." She did not look at the Andean suits when she said it, but she angled the display so they could see.

Dov was already moving. He braced his boots against the rungs, lit the plasma cutter, and drew it down the panel in a long white seam that threw sparks drifting slow and red through the low gravity. The smell of scorched alloy came through the suit filters. One of the Pacific men started to crowd in behind him to hold the panel, and one of Isa's riggers shouldered forward to do the same, and for a moment the two of them hung there with their hands on the same edge of metal. Isa pushed between them. "You take the top, you take the bottom," she said, and pointed, and they did. The panel came free. She sent the Pacific scanner team in first because they knew the route and her own people second because they had the tools, and she went last, counting helmets as they passed her lamp.

The bypass was a crawl through ribbed piping slick with condensate, and twice the line stalled when someone's tank snagged on a bracket. Each time Isa called the next name, Andean or Pacific without distinction, until the line moved again. Dov burned through the second panel with his visor fogging, and they spilled out into the reactor access corridor, a long white throat of a room lined with shielding plates and humming with a deep, uneven vibration that came up through the deck into their bones. At the far end, framed in the open core housing, Vance knelt with a torque driver in his hand. The first retaining bolt was already out and turning lazily in the air beside him, and as the eleven of them came through the gap he set the driver to the second and began to turn.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="4101ba09-31be-4391-b60e-ff6e918f8158">Above the shafts, the Andean tug and the Pacific craft stayed fastened to the flanks of 2041 KX.</li>
<li edge="2ecac8d9-2f7a-4391-917c-c94c7afd3781">At the far end of the corridor, Dov watched Vance set the driver to the second bolt.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0460b054-edf2-43f3-9f4e-7928b9e9b7e6" -->

The command deck of Halcyon Station was built in a ring around a sunken well of screens, and on the night the two tugs locked their clamps onto 2041 KX, every officer stood at the rail looking down into it. On the largest display the asteroid turned slowly, a lump of grey ore streaked with pale veins, with the tugs fixed to its flanks like ticks. Their thrusters burned in long, patient pulses, and the numbers beside them climbed toward high orbit by small, stubborn degrees. Around that image the feeds from Earth flickered in their smaller frames: Lima at dusk with whole districts going black, then Manila, then São Paulo, then Osaka, the lights failing block by block as the lithium storage gave out beneath them.

The audio came in fragments over the station's speakers. A minister of the Andean council, her face washed blue by a backup lamp, demanded that the Pacific crew cut the asteroid loose and leave its metal to those who had first claimed it. Before she finished, the Pacific bloc's answer arrived on another channel: without that metal its own grid would collapse inside a season, and it would not let go. The officers listened without speaking. A young navigator gripped the rail until his knuckles went white as the city behind each speaker vanished into darkness, so that the two blocs seemed to be arguing from inside the same night.

Then a third signal broke through, carrying the seals of both blocs side by side, something none of the officers had seen before. News of Governor Vance's rationing had reached the surface, and neither bloc would wait to see where it led. The order was read out in a clerk's flat voice: the governor was to surrender the station's lithium reserves at once and hold himself ready to receive envoys, who would settle the asteroid's ownership and appoint a new governor of the colonies before 2041 KX reached its final orbit. It ended with a docking schedule and no closing courtesy.

Vance had heard it from the head of the well, his hands folded behind his back. When the signal cut out he stood still for a long moment, and a muscle worked along his jaw. "They send for me," he said at last, "as if I kept their ledgers." He turned to face the ring of officers. He told them that Earth had left the colonies to freeze and starve through every crisis of the last twenty years, that the blocs remembered Halcyon only when they wanted something out of the sky, and that not one envoy on that schedule had ever drawn breath behind a hull. "They abandoned us," he said. "Now they want to pick our ruler. No one down there is fit to govern this station, and no one they send will be either. I am, and I will."

Nobody answered him. He stepped down to the main console and keyed in his command codes, and one by one the flight records of the lost convoy closed under his seal: the telemetry, the voice logs, the final seconds before the ships broke apart. Red locks spread across the archive panel until every entry carried his name and no other. On the big screen above him the asteroid kept turning in the grip of the tugs, rising toward the station a little at a time, while the cities on the smaller feeds went on going dark.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c7e3f85a-1248-479b-b58c-2247610a6868">When the envoys docked on schedule, Vance went down to receive them at the coolant junction of Threnody station.</li>
<li edge="7c4a0f97-6f16-44e2-988b-95f72db9ba3d">Before the envoys could dock, Vance climbed up to the communications array of the shipyard control spindle.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="41370eca-3b91-4c94-97b5-e85f3faf150d" -->

The coolant junction of Threnody station was a ring of pipe-crowded steel, warm as a held breath, and through its single viewport the two tugs could be seen hauling 2041 KX toward high Earth orbit, their tethers drawn taut across the dark like the strings of a bow. The envoys stood on either side of the junction's grated floor. Neither had brought a chair. The Meridian envoy spoke first and said the asteroid could not be divided, because half its nickel and iridium would carry the Meridian grid through two winters and fail it in the third. The Halvorsen envoy said the same of her own grid, nearly word for word, and neither of them seemed to notice the echo. Their wrist terminals chimed within seconds of each other. In both capitals the orders had already gone out: station modules undocked, armed tugs launched, each bloc to take its own end of the rock by force. Vance, the station's overseer, stood between them with his hands clasped behind his back and did not say a thing.

Then the reactor groaned somewhere below, a low structural note that rose through the soles of everyone's boots, and the blast doors came down. They fell from the ceiling on both sides of the junction with a sound like a dropped anvil, sealing in the envoys, Vance, Dov from reactor maintenance, two engineers from the rival blocs, and a lean woman named Ines Corra who had flown Vance's shuttle until the year before. Red light flooded the ring. A panel announced in a flat voice that the core housing was cracked and the coolant pressure was falling.

Dov knew the seal sequence the way other men knew prayers, and he called it out in a steady voice: valve four, then nine, then the cross-feed on the lower manifold, not the upper, never the upper. The Meridian engineer and the Halvorsen engineer had each laid lines through Threnody's guts that appeared on no shared schematic, private routes cut for private reasons, and now they called those routes to one another across the junction, a man in a grey jumpsuit and a woman in a blue one, each opening valves that only the other could close. Neither stopped to ask whether the other was lying. Above them a bulkhead plate began to bow inward with a shriek of tearing rivets, and Ines Corra put her shoulder and both palms against it and held it there. Her arms shook, and frost crept across the steel around her fingers, but the plate did not move.

The pressure needle steadied, then climbed. A maintenance hatch at the junction's far side hissed and swung open on a narrow passage, lit amber, that led down toward the cracked core, and the engineers looked at each other in plain surprise that it had worked. The envoys had pressed themselves against the wall with their terminals still glowing. Only Vance went the other way. He stepped through the service door to the control room before anyone thought to stop him, and the door sealed behind him with a clean, deliberate click. Through its small window his face showed pale and composed in the red light, and he did not look back at the people he had left in the junction.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="431e72e6-001e-4c00-ba25-7bbd9b4580d6">Beyond the viewport, the crews of the Halcyon Ring crowded into their commons to watch the night fall over the equatorial strait.</li>
<li edge="9a97ec95-b09a-427b-85bf-1889ba77d635">Behind the sealed door, Vance turned from the window toward the controls that still answered to the reactor below.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="d378b035-3e52-47a5-97b9-fdf890765a3c" -->

On the governor's command deck of Halcyon Station, the forward display showed 2041 KX turning slowly against the black, a dull lump of iron and nickel the length of a city, belted at its waist by the docking ring that had been built to hold it. At its eastern end the Meridian Compact's station had already sealed its clamps; at the western end the Pacific Accord's was still settling, thrusters flaring in short, careful bursts. Both had run their colors up on the hull lights, red-and-white at one end and blue at the other, as if the rock were a field that could be claimed by walking onto it. Between them the tugs hung in loose formation, and the targeting overlays had turned every one of them amber. Below, the curve of Earth filled the lower third of the screen, and somewhere on that bright crescent was the salt flat where this quarrel had begun. Now it had been lifted into a place where neither side could step back without falling.

Marcus Vance stood at the center of the deck with his hands folded behind him, the way he had stood for every broadcast that had made him governor of the colonies. He did not look at the flags. He looked at Halcyon's own schematic, at the reactor core glowing a steady green beneath the habitat spine, and said in the same even voice, "Commander Osei. Disable the containment governors and bring the core to one hundred and forty percent. If either of them boards us, they inherit a crater."

No one moved. Osei stood at the engineering station with one hand resting flat on the console, and for a moment the only sounds on the deck were the ventilators and the soft chime of a proximity alert that nobody had silenced. Then she turned back to the board and typed a short sequence without hurrying. Beside her, Lieutenant Havel did the same, and after him the communications officer, and the navigator, each of them entering a code and pressing a palm to the reader in turn. The schematic flickered. A gray banner slid across the top of Vance's command display: AUTHORITY SUSPENDED — QUORUM OVERRIDE. The reactor stayed green.

"You don't have the authority," Vance said. He pressed his own palm to the reader at his station, then pressed it again, harder, and the panel answered him both times with the same flat tone. He gave the order a second time, louder, and then a third, naming each officer, and each officer kept working and did not look up. Osei opened a channel to both docked stations and began, in a voice as calm as his had been, to explain that Halcyon would not be firing on anyone. On the forward screen the amber overlays on the nearest tugs went white, one after another.

Vance stayed where he was, at the center of the deck, still governor of the colonies under every charter that had ever been signed. Around him the officers moved between their stations, stepping past him and around him to reach their consoles, the way people step around a support column. The banner glowed above his empty controls. Outside, the two flags hung at opposite ends of the asteroid, Earth turned beneath them, and no one on the deck turned to see what the governor would do next.

<!-- cell: markdown id="841380c1-ede5-4492-89aa-33f88ec54bba" -->

The Halcyon slid into the Kessler Belt with her running lights dimmed, a narrow hull threading the long graveyard that circled Threnody. Outside the forward viewport, the dead of a hundred old engagements turned slowly in the dark: split tanker spines, a radar dish folded like a hand, a cloud of bolts that glittered when the ship's floodlamps swept across it. On the bridge, the Council's evening broadcast played on the overhead screen, a calm voice over footage of a long white table. *The peace delegation at Threnody,* the voice said, *represents the best of what the Assembly can still become. Its envoy has shown patience where others have shown only ambition.* No name was spoken, and none needed to be. Commander Vance stood beneath the screen with his arms folded, and the muscle along his jaw worked as though he were chewing something hard.

He did not look away from the broadcast while he reached for the console beside his chair. Lieutenant Oyelaran's personnel file was already open there, the clearance seal on its header a fraction too bright, its authorization chain ending in a signature from an office that had been dissolved two years earlier. Vance copied the file to the private drive on his wrist, watched the transfer bar fill, and closed the window. Then he turned his head. "Lieutenant." Oyelaran, at the sensor station, went still. "My quarters. Twenty-two hundred. Come alone." She said, "Yes, sir," in a voice with nothing in it, and bent back over her readouts. Her hands had stopped moving on the keys.

"Contact, two points high, drifting across." Lio leaned toward the forward glass, calling the wreckage the way a lookout on an older sea would have called rocks. "Hull plate, big one, tumbling. Another behind it, low and left." At the helm, Isa breathed out through her teeth and rolled the Halcyon onto her side. A slab of armor the size of a cargo lift scraped past the starboard lights close enough to show the rivets. Yuna pressed a hand to her headset. "Commander, Dov's on the line from engineering. He says the main coupling's running forty degrees over and climbing. He wants us under half thrust or he can't promise it holds."

Vance looked at the plot. The rival ship was a small amber mark on the far edge of the display, closer to Threnody than it had been an hour before, and the white table on the broadcast was still waiting for whoever arrived first. "Tell Dov to keep it together," he said. Isa started to answer, and he spoke over her. "Full speed. Now." For a moment nobody on the bridge moved. Then Isa pushed the throttle forward, the deck began to hum underfoot, and the Halcyon leapt ahead, straight into the thickest drift of the Belt. Ahead of her the wreckage closed in, dense and turning, and Lio began calling contacts faster than anyone could answer them.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e8de79ba-6967-4da5-8090-1f44c9594055">Lieutenant Oyelaran went to the Commander's private quarters at twenty-two hundred, alone, as she had been told.</li>
<li edge="d967c919-1077-4b24-97c8-ad465a164b4f">Lio, Isa, Yuna and Dov turned their eyes to the shipyard gantries of the Halcyon Ring, where the Belt's wreckage drifted close.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2e288e04-c350-4415-94d1-3d4c3ea9eaa9" -->

The salvage corridor of Threnody station ran a quarter mile through the dark, its deck plates buckled where some old collision had folded the hull inward, and the only light came from work lamps strung along the conduits like beads on a wire. Vance's flagship sat clamped to the far lock, its running lights dimmed. At the corridor's midpoint, where a blast door had jammed half-open, the crew found the other faction's engineers already at work: six of them in patched suits, faces grey with recycled air and too many shifts. Nobody reached for a weapon. A woman with a cracked visor lowered her cutting torch, looked at the newcomers a long moment, and said only that the coffee in their tender was terrible but there was enough of it.

Lio ended up cross-legged on the deck beside their navigator, a thin man named Edrey, the two of them spreading charts across an upturned crate. Edrey's maps showed drift lanes through the Belt that appeared on no bloc survey, and Lio traded him the flagship's long-range soundings in exchange, both of them marking corrections in grease pencil and arguing happily over a rock field neither had dared to cross. Further along, Dov and the rival chief, a broad, slow-spoken woman called Hesk, pooled their kits on a tarp. Dov's torque drivers fit fittings her crew had been forcing for weeks; her hydraulic spreaders were the only tools on the station that could open the shielding collar. By the end of the shift they had traced the reactor core's mounting together and seen the shape of the problem plainly: the core was locked behind interlocks on two sides of the corridor, each needing a team, and the shielding would only hold if both were released at the same instant. Neither crew could free it alone.

In the flagship's command cabin, Vance sat with the stolen cipher keys spread across his console and composed dispatches. He wrote them carefully, one in the c

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="931bd41f-8f73-4ccc-b9d0-febe9c6dbcc2">Vance left the cipher keys on his console and walked down to the flagship's brig.</li>
<li edge="6063efc9-5b98-4f50-bd41-95b44c730995">Teodora climbed the docking spine to the Meridian ration office above the reactor bay.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="890a6a25-d39c-48cd-a2b6-d224a1d47db8" -->

The medical bay of Halcyon Station smelled of antiseptic and scorched insulation, and through its long viewport the convoy's wreckage turned slowly against the black: a torn hull plate, a tumbling cargo pod, a cloud of glittering debris that caught the planet's light and let it go again. Yuna moved between the cots with her sleeves pushed to the elbow, closing a burn on one rival engineer's forearm, splinting another's wrist, never once asking which company's insignia had been stitched to the ruined jackets piled by the door. The engineers watched her work with the wary silence of people who had expected to be left to drift.

It was the eldest of them, a gray-bearded woman with a bandage across her temple, who broke it. She drew a cracked data slate from her coat and set it on the instrument tray beside Yuna's hand. "You pulled us out," she said. "That's owed." The scans bloomed across the screen in false color: the station's reactor core, its containment lattice threaded with hairline fractures, three of them pulsing an angry red where the stress had gathered. Isa leaned in first, then Dov, then Lio, and for a long moment none of them spoke. Dov traced the fractures with a fingertip and began, half under his breath, to describe the sealing sequence he had been sketching for weeks, and the rival woman nodded at every step, then stopped him at the second fracture and told him exactly why it would fail there. By the time she had finished, the truth sat plainly in the room. They knew where the core would break; Dov knew how to hold it together. Neither crew could reach inside that reactor without the other. Isa said it aloud, and the rivals agreed: the salvage bay, once the wounded could stand, to plan it together.

The hatch hissed open before anyone could say more. The colonial assembly came through it in a frightened knot, a dozen delegates in rumpled civic sashes, some still clutching the emergency placards they had snatched from the corridors, and they pressed into the narrow space between the cots until Yuna had to shield a patient with her body. Their speaker, a thin man with trembling hands, did not look at the engineers or the scans. He looked at Vance, who had stood the whole while against the far bulkhead with his arms folded. The station needed one voice, the speaker said; the Council was too far away and too slow, and the wreckage outside the glass was proof of what slowness cost. Would Vance take emergency command?

Vance let the question hang until the murmuring died. Then he told them he would, on two conditions: that the assembly renounce the Council's authority over Halcyon, here and on the record, and that every one of them swear no one but he would speak for the station. A few delegates flinched, and one opened his mouth to object, but the debris drifted past the viewport again and the objection never came. They swore, one after another, their voices thin in the cramped bay. When the last of them had shuffled out and Yuna had gone back to her stitching, Vance crossed the room and stopped at Dov's shoulder. He did not raise his voice. "Your seal plans," he said. "Bring them to me. Only to me." Then he was gone through the hatch, and Dov stood motionless beside the glowing slate, its red fractures still pulsing in his hands.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a687368d-088b-4ed4-aeda-fcf68c03bbff">Vance climbed to the command deck to take up the seat the assembly had just handed him.</li>
<li edge="21b457c4-6ebd-4e33-9044-9099d736c40f">Dov carried his seal plans, still unshown to Vance, down to the ice hauler dock where the two crews would meet.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="6b9fea4b-9a22-4843-861b-e1a45799d7e6" -->

The reactor corridor of Halcyon Station's life-support depot ran forty meters from the ration lockers to the core housing, and on that day it was full of people. Through the long viewport at its far end, the wreckage of the supply convoy turned slowly against the blue limb of the planet, cargo pods tumbling open and spilling frozen water into orbit like scattered salt. Below the port, colonists shoved one another toward the lockers, children held up to show how thin they had grown, men pulling oxygen canisters out of each other's hands. Vance stood on the locker crates with a sidearm on his hip and the depot's master keys on a cord around his neck. He spoke over them in a level voice. The reserves, he told them, belonged to Halcyon now, and Halcyon needed a governor. Every name that swore to him would draw a ration. Every name that did not would draw nothing. His officers stood along the bulkhead in their faded convoy jackets and kept their faces still while the first oaths were spoken and the first canisters handed down. A woman who refused was turned away with empty hands, and none of them moved to stop it.

When the lockers were sealed again and the sworn had carried their portions out, Vance walked the length of the corridor to the core housing alone. Dov called after him from the bulkhead. The engineer had spent eleven years on depots like this one and knew the housing's seals by their order and their temper, and he said so. Vance did not turn around. He set the extraction clamps himself, read the manual off a cracked wall panel, and threw the release in the order the panel gave. The third seal let go before the second. A sound like a struck bell rang through the deck plates, then a thin, rising hiss, and the amber lights along the housing went red one after another down the length of the corridor.

Vance came back up the corridor at a run with the master keys in his fist. He passed his officers, and he passed the six engineers from the rival crew who had been pulled out of their dying tug and brought aboard, and he reached the blast door controls at the corridor's mouth. He looked back once at the red housing, the core still seated in its cradle and still his, and he keyed the doors shut from the far side. They came down in two heavy sections and locked with a pressure seal that thumped in everyone's ears. The corridor lights dropped to emergency strips. The hiss went on.

For a moment the people left inside stood where they were: Vance's former crew along one wall, the rescued engineers along the other, the length of the deck between them marked by dropped ration wrappers and a single lost glove. The rival crew's chief, a broad woman named Teodora with burn scars across both hands, was the first to walk toward the housing. Dov met her halfway. He told her plainly that he knew the seals and did not know the coolant manifold of this model. She told him she knew the manifold and had never touched a seal like these. Behind them the others began to come forward, a pilot who could reroute power by hand, a medic who could read the dosimeters, two welders from the tug with their torches still clipped to their belts. Nobody made a speech. They gathered at the red housing in the low light, opened the panels between them, and began to say aloud, one after another, what each of them could do.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8b286d11-c6ef-453e-898c-6c5dd645de1b">Beyond the sealed blast doors, Vance went up to the command deck, where his officers stood watching the feeds from Earth.</li>
<li edge="e83a1e92-edda-407a-9c3a-b9377fc09e38">Inside, Teodora led the gathered crews up the central spoke toward the spindle's sealed hatch.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="71ed06d3-d90e-427d-a661-7a0363ea0efd" -->

The broadcast reached Halcyon Station on every open band at once: the bloc fleets, scorched and limping at the edge of the debris field, had pulled Admiral Vance's forged transmissions apart word by word and laid the seams bare for anyone to read. Any officer who delivered him, the message said, would be granted amnesty. In the command ring men looked at their hands and did not look at each other. Vance listened to the whole of it standing, his collar still fastened to the throat, and when it ended he did not answer the fleets. He keyed the station-wide channel instead and ordered the reactor's safety limits stripped, every governor and interlock, and in a voice as level as a ruler he told Halcyon that if he could not hold it, it would burn, and no one else would ever sit where he sat.

His chief engineer, Halvard Sun, said "Yes, Admiral," and bent over the console. While Vance watched the core temperature climb on the main display like a man watching a tide he had summoned, Sun's fingers moved through a second set of commands beneath the first, quietly unhooking the master controls from the bridge and threading them out to the engineering decks, one relay at a time. He was three relays short when the core cracked. The sound came up through the deck plating as a single low note, and the antechamber beyond the reactor filled with a pale, glittering haze that the dosimeters greeted with a scream.

The manual seal had been built by people who distrusted any one pair of hands: three valves, set at opposite sides of the chamber, which had to turn at the same instant or not at all. Sun had saved the third to a remote actuator before the bridge locked him out. The other two required bodies. Ilse Marrow, an engineer who had spent four years calling Sun's work sloppy in every review that would print it, and Teo Ashgrove, who had flown Vance's personal shuttle until the week he refused to fly it again, went through the hatch together on a single shared tether, because the second one had melted on its reel. Over the comm, from a shielded alcove two decks up, Dov's voice set the rhythm: "In. Hold. Move. In. Hold. Move." They crawled to it, elbows and knees on the hot grating, the haze sparking in their visor lights like snow in a headlamp.

On the bridge Vance saw two shapes crossing his reactor and understood at once. He opened the antechamber's vents. The air left in a long rushing exhale that dragged at their suits and snapped the tether taut between them, and Teo's suit, patched and old, began to whistle. Ilse had the only working mask. She pulled it from her face, pressed it against his for three of Dov's counts, took it back for three more, and passed it again, and in that way they went on, breathing in turns, the tether drawing them together and apart across the bleeding chamber. "Hold," said Dov. "Hold. Now." Ilse's gloves closed on the first wheel, Teo's on the second, Sun's actuator whined somewhere in the dark, and the first valve, rimed and shrieking, began to turn.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="dedaf88a-e52e-4325-8ebc-eecaed1856da">On the command deck, Vance turned back to his controls as the bloc stations closed on asteroid 2041 KX.</li>
<li edge="01021063-c18c-4e01-bedf-de9d09eee510">In the reactor core beneath the control spindle, Teodora and the crews bent to the remaining valves.</li>
</ol>

